var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Objects,-Inheritance-and-Modules-in-Julia","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Julia is an amazing programming language that finds its use mainly in the hands of scientific modelers. Julia combines a welcoming syntax that doesn't scare new users with stellar performance rivaling C++ and Fortran. While Julia is easy to get into – the basics are a hybrid between Python and Matlab – there are some quirks to the language that change the way you need to think about overall program architecture: there are no objects. However, don't be discouraged: there is a reason for everything. I'll explain not only how we can work around the lack of objects, but also to embrace it! Last but not least, I present a new module to plug the final hole in the sea of abstractions that is left by the absence of objects: mixins, but more on that later.","title":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#The-JIT-compiler","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Julia aims to be both user-friendly and fast to execute. To achieve this, we get the wonderful mix of a dynamically typed language that is compiled to machine instructions, just-in-time (JIT). This works as follows: when a function is called, we know the types of the arguments (the type signature) that are given as input, and the compiler generates optimized code for that specific type signature. When the function is called again with the same signature, the compiled version is reused from cache.","title":"The JIT compiler"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Multiple-dispatch","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Since the type signature is such an integral part of the execution model, there is a nice trick we can play: multiple dispatch. We can redefine the same method for many different type signatures (similar to function overloading in C++). For instance, the addition operator has (as of Julia 1.10) 189 method implementations. The cute thing is that multiple dispatch emerged directly from the choice of having a dynamically typed JIT-compiled language. The developers of the Julia language decided to go all-in on this emergent aspect of the language and embrace it as an important means of abstraction.","title":"Multiple dispatch"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Multiple-dispatch-supersedes-objects","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Multiple dispatch leads us to our first comparison with object oriented languages: abstract method calls are a dispatch on just the first parameter (self or this). In this respect the level of abstraction that multiple dispatch offers is more powerful than the idea of tying method implementations to compound types (i.e. objects). However, object oriented programming is more than just objects. Let's see how Julia compares.","title":"Multiple dispatch supersedes objects"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Object-Oriented-Programming","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Object Oriented Programming as we know it today is a group of abstractions guided around the principle of having some way to dynamically look-up a method implementation for some object. I know this skips over the origin and abstract concepts around objects as they were found in the Smalltalk language, but that is besides the point. OOP as we know it is mostly designed around a mostly  antiquated run-time involving pointers and vtables. What I'm getting at, is that the abstractions in a language are often very much guided by the underlying run-time implementation. That means that in C++ the idea of classes makes sense. In Python we already have a very different view of an object, as everything is based around objects being hashmaps with some commonly understood interface. In Julia, having such a different run-time, multiple dispatch makes sense. Quite similar to the idea that the kind of music we make or hear depends on the setting in which it is staged (opera house without electric amplification, a living room setting, a noisy café or earbuds on a daily commute) and the available technology, more so than other cultural considerations. Meanwhile, we have entire schools of thought on how to organise code and design architectures around larger code bases. These ideas have been heavily influenced by the tools of the time: Java and C++. So, all that considered, what do we understand by Object Oriented Programming?\n\nCompartimenting program state: data hiding, modularization\nMessage passing between objects: similar to above, an object's behaviour can be completely understood from the way we poke sticks at it.\nAbstraction over interfaces: the interface is the outer shell of an object. If the implementations are widely different but the interface is the same, we can freely interchange objects of different types in cases of heterogeneous data.\nInheritance or composition: we can use smaller objects to build larger ones. This can be done by inheritance, whereby the larger object behaves the same as the smaller one, except it can do more. The other way is by composition: we wrap the smaller object into the larger one, defining a new interface, but retaining the functionality.\n\nIn Julia we can achieve all these goals with multiple dispatch, except inheritance. Keep in mind that Julia is a dynamically typed language. Not only that, it fully embraces being dynamically typed through the dispatch mechanism.","title":"Object Oriented Programming"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Functions,-Methods,-Interfaces","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Ok, now we know: Julia doesn't have classes. How do we then organize our code? What are the means of abstraction? A common pattern is to define methods around types with similar utility.\n\nHere we see that we can make methods that are in the standard library operate on our own custom types.\n\nThe weakness in this approach is that none of this is checked at compile time. Also, this mechanism is sometimes abused, where people overload a single function from Base, just because it seems nice. Often this practice will lead to less readable code. Always be aware that functions inside Base are often part of a larger interface, an unspoken contract with the developer. Julia would become a much nicer language if there was a way to enforce such a contract.\n\nFor instance, if you implement the + operator on an object, you should really think: does my operator commute? Can I define a zero(::Type{T}) along with it? If your operation does not commute, it may be better to implement * and one instead. Or, if there is no identity element, implement your own function and give it a good name.","title":"Functions, Methods, Interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Composition-over-Inheritance","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Suppose we're developing something of a graphics library. We have defined a type to work with points:\n\nstruct Point\n    x::Float64\n    y::Float64\nend\n\nBase.:+(a::Point, b::Point) = Point(a.x+b.x, a.y+b.y)\n\nNow, we want to add colour to our points. We have a colour type that stores colour as an RGB triple. For convenience, we'll even throw in an abstract type AbstractColour\n\nstruct Colour\n    r::Float64\n    g::Float64\n    b::Float64\nend\n\nWe could do the following:\n\nstruct ColouredPoint\n    x::Float64\n    y::Float64\n    colour::Colour\nend\n\nFor the Point type we had defined an addition operator. How would you define that for the ColouredPoint? How do we handle the colour information?\n\nIn this example it is quite obvious that the better other option is to use composition.\n\nstruct ColouredPoint\n    point::Point\n    colour::Colour\nend\n\nIn general, for most cases it is considered best practice to prefer composition over inheritance. Good for us, since Julia does not implement inheritance.","title":"Composition over Inheritance"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Critique-against-OOP","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"","title":"Critique against OOP"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Composing-Modules","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Now I present another example which requires a different form of inheritance, and is not so easily expressed using object composition (of the kind shown above). Moreover, we'll see that we can combine the idea of an interface in Julia with that of a module, creating something that works much the same as a class in other languages.\n\nWe need to get an overview of the problem: we'll implement a simple physics model, first without any abstraction. Then we'll use modules and mixins to restructure that code. Along the way you'll learn an interesting fact about physical modelling. We will model a bouncing weight on a spring. The spring exerts a force proportional to the position of the weight.\n\n(Image: Sketch of our model)\n\nSuch a system is described by having a force proportional and opposite to the relative position of the weight. To model this system, we need to keep track of both the velocity and the position of the object.\n\nOur model has an Input structure to store all the parameters, and a State structure that is updated on every iteration.\n\nflowchart LR;\n    init(\"initial state\")\n    check{\"t &lt; t<sub>end</sub>?\"}\n    step![\"advance model\"]\n    done(\"end program\")\n\n    init --> step! --> check\n    check -- true --> step!\n    check -- false --> done\n\nFirst of all, the input structure: we need to specify the properties of the spring (spring constant, and weight of the moving object), the specifics of our time integration (in this case, the time step and final time), and the initial conditions. In a larger model there will be many more parameters.\n\n#| id: just-a-spring\nusing Unitful\n\n@kwdef struct Input\n    initial_position::typeof(1.0u\"m\")\n    spring_constant::typeof(1.0u\"N/m\")\n    mass::typeof(1.0u\"kg\")\n    time_step::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    time_end::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\nend\n\nA typical model will have some mutable State.\n\n#| id: just-a-spring\n@kwdef mutable struct State\n    time::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    position::typeof(1.0u\"m\")\n    velocity::typeof(1.0u\"m/s\")\nend\n\nThe first state is created from the given input. Here, we only configure the initial position of the spring and hard-coded the initial velocity to zero.\n\n#| id: just-a-spring\ninit(input::Input) =\n    State(time = 0.0u\"s\", position = input.initial_position, velocity = 0.0u\"m/s\")\n\nThen we step! over this state, until some condition is met.\n\n#| id: just-a-spring\nfunction step!(input::Input, state::State)\n    Δt = input.time_step\n    Δx = state.velocity * Δt\n    Δv = -state.position * input.spring_constant / input.mass * Δt\n\n    state.time += Δt\n    state.position += Δx\n    state.velocity += Δv\nend\n\nWe'll go ahead and write the main loop in a generic way.","title":"Composing Modules"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Modules-as-classes","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"In Julia, modules can be passed around much the same as integers or functions [1]. Given that we expect a module to have an init and step! function as well as a State type, we can write a generic function for running our model.\n\n[1]: That is, passed around as arguments to functions. However, modules cannot be created dynamically.\n\nWe put the previous code inside a module called Spring and then write a run function that turns a module and input record into a sequence of output values.\n\n#| id: spring-run-slow\nfunction run(model::Module, input)\n    state = model.init(input)\n    Channel{model.State}() do ch\n        while state.time < input.time_end\n            model.step!(input, state)\n            put!(ch, deepcopy(state))\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nThere is a problem with this code: it is very slow. The model.init and model.step! items are resolved through dynamic look-up. We can make it a lot faster by forcing the compiler to specialize for every model we give it.\n\n#| id: spring-run-fast\nstruct Model{T} end\n\nfunction run(::Type{Model{M}}, input) where M\n    state = M.init(input)\n    Channel{M.State}() do ch\n        while state.time < input.time_end\n            M.step!(input, state)\n            put!(ch, deepcopy(state))\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nLet's see what this does, by visualizing the output. Here, we also show the energy of the system.\n\n#| id: just-a-spring\nfunction energy(input::Input, state::State)\n    k = state.velocity^2 * input.mass / 2\n    v = state.position^2 * input.spring_constant / 2\n    return k + v\nend\n\ndetails: Plotting code\n#| id: spring-plot\nfunction plot_result(input, output, energy)\n    times = [f.time for f in output]\n    pos = [f.position for f in output]\n\n    fig = Figure()\n    ax1 = Axis(fig[1:2, 1];\n        ylabel = \"position\",\n        dim1_conversion = Makie.UnitfulConversion(u\"s\"),\n        dim2_conversion = Makie.UnitfulConversion(u\"m\"),\n    )\n    lines!(ax1, times, pos)\n    ax2 = Axis(fig[3, 1];\n        ylabel = \"energy\",\n        dim1_conversion = Makie.UnitfulConversion(u\"s\"),\n        dim2_conversion = Makie.UnitfulConversion(u\"J\"),\n    )\n    lines!(ax2, times, energy)\n    fig\nend#| file: examples/just_a_spring.jl\n#| classes: [\"task\"]\n#| creates:\n#|   - docs/src/fig/just-a-spring.svg\n#| collect: figures\nmodule Spring\n    <<just-a-spring>>\nend\n\nmodule Script\n    using Unitful\n    using CairoMakie\n    using ..Spring\n\n    <<spring-run-fast>>\n    <<spring-plot>>\n\n    function main()\n        input = Spring.Input(\n            time_step = 0.01u\"s\",\n            time_end = 5.0u\"s\",\n            spring_constant = 50.0u\"N/m\",\n            initial_position = 1.0u\"m\",\n            mass = 1.0u\"kg\",\n        )\n\n        output = run(Model{Spring}, input) |> collect\n        energy = [Spring.energy(input, s) for s in output]\n        fig = plot_result(input, output, energy)\n        save(\"docs/src/fig/just-a-spring.svg\", fig)\n    end\nend\n\nScript.main()\n\ninfo: Simulation of a spring\n(Image: Output of our spring simulation)Our simulation is running, but there appears to be something wrong here! Why is the amplitude of the oscillation increasing? Remember, we set the initial velocity to 0. After one step of forward Euler integration we have a velocity v  0, but the position is still the same, so the system gets a boost. The integration method is not energy conserving.\n\nNow that we're familiar with the problem, let's try to deconstruct this program into multiple components.","title":"Modules as classes"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#With-Mixins","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Now, we implement the same model using ModuleMixins.jl.\n\nWe define some common types:\n\n#| id: mixin-a-spring\nmodule Common\n    export AbstractInput, AbstractState, Model, run\n\n    abstract type AbstractInput end\n    abstract type AbstractState end\n\n    <<spring-run-fast>>\nend\n\nWe split our concerns into a Time module and a Spring module. Both these modules define an Input and State struct as well as the init and step! functions.\n\n#| id: mixin-a-spring\n@compose module Time\n    using Unitful\n    using ..Common\n\n    @kwdef struct Input <: AbstractInput\n        time_step::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n        time_end::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    end\n\n    @kwdef mutable struct State <: AbstractState\n        time::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    end\n\n    function init(input::AbstractInput)\n        State(time = 0.0u\"s\")\n    end\n\n    function step!(input::AbstractInput, state::AbstractState; fraction::Float64 = 1.0)\n        state.time += fraction * input.time_step\n    end\nend\n\nAlthough the Time model doesn't compute anything interesting, we could call Model.run(Time, Time.Input(0.1u\"s\", 1.0u\"s\")), and collect a series of time coordinates. There is something satisfying about being able to build out complexity from humble beginnings. In the Spring module, we extend the Input and State definitions present in Time and recreate the oscillator model we had before.\n\n#| id: mixin-a-spring\n@compose module Spring\n    @mixin Time\n    using Unitful\n    using ..Common\n\n    @kwdef struct Input <: AbstractInput\n        initial_position::typeof(1.0u\"m\")\n        spring_constant::typeof(1.0u\"N/m\")\n        mass::typeof(1.0u\"kg\")\n    end\n\n    @kwdef mutable struct State <: AbstractState\n        position::typeof(1.0u\"m\")\n        velocity::typeof(1.0u\"m/s\")\n    end\n\n    accelleration(input::AbstractInput, state::AbstractState) =\n        -state.position * input.spring_constant / input.mass\n\n    energy(input::AbstractInput, state::AbstractState) =\n        let k = state.velocity^2 * input.mass / 2,\n            v = state.position^2 * input.spring_constant / 2\n            k + v\n        end\n\n    step!(input::AbstractInput, state::AbstractState) =\n        let a = accelleration(input, state)\n            state.position += state.velocity * input.time_step\n            state.velocity += a * input.time_step\n            Time.step!(input, state)\n        end\n\n    init(input::AbstractInput) =\n        State(time = 0.0u\"s\", position = input.initial_position, velocity = 0.0u\"m/s\")\nend","title":"With Mixins"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Using-a-better-integrator","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"In the first part we saw that there were some shortcomings to the step! method, in that energy was not conserved. The integration method we used is also known as the forward Euler method. There is a better way integrate systems for which energy is conserved, known as the leap-frog method. The idea is to compute the change in velocity and the change in position at times offset by half a time step. These partial step methods are known as kick! (changing the velocity) and drift! (changing the position).\n\n#| id: mixin-a-spring\nmodule LeapFrog\n    using ..Common\n    using ..Time\n\n    function leap_frog(::Type{Model{M}}) where M\n        function (input::AbstractInput, state::AbstractState)\n            M.kick!(input, state)\n            Time.step!(input, state; fraction = 0.5)\n            M.drift!(input, state)\n            Time.step!(input, state; fraction = 0.5)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nNow, let's see if we can extend our previous implementation of Spring to work with the new integrator. Notice that we can import methods from Spring for reuse.\n\n#| id: mixin-a-spring\n@compose module LeapFrogSpring\n    @mixin Spring\n    using ..Common\n    using ..Spring: energy, init, accelleration\n    using ..LeapFrog\n\n    Base.convert(::Type{State}, s::Spring.State) =\n        State(time=s.time, position=s.position, velocity=s.velocity)\n\n    kick!(input::AbstractInput, state::AbstractState) =\n        state.velocity += accelleration(input, state) * input.time_step\n\n    drift!(input::AbstractInput, state::AbstractState; fraction::Float64=1.0) =\n        state.position += state.velocity * input.time_step * fraction\n\n    const step! = LeapFrog.leap_frog(Model{LeapFrogSpring})\nend\n\nnote: Why the `convert` function?\nNote that the Spring.init function will return a value Spring.State. However, the Model.run function expects a LeapFrogSpring.State instead. I may patch ModuleMixins to create a type alias and not define a new identical type when nothing changes. For the moment, a convert function fixes the situation.\n\nnote: What did we use here?\nAlso note that we didn't do anything strange here. The only thing we used from ModuleMixins is the composition and inheritance of struct definitions. The rest is just using standard Julia modules creatively. Not only did we see inheritance in action, the LeapFrog module could be seen as a transformer, taking one interface (having kick! and drift!), and turning it into another one (generating a step!).\n\ninfo: Result from the Leap-frog integrator\n(Image: Result from the Leap-frog integration)\n\ndetails: Plotting code\n#| file: examples/mixin_a_spring.jl\n#| classes: [\"task\"]\n#| creates:\n#|   - docs/src/fig/mixin-a-spring.svg\n#| collect: figures\nusing ModuleMixins: @compose\n\n<<mixin-a-spring>>\n\nmodule Script\n    using Unitful\n    using CairoMakie\n    using ModuleMixins\n\n    using ..Time\n    using ..Spring\n    using ..Common\n    using ..LeapFrogSpring\n\n    <<spring-plot>>\n\n    function main()\n        input = LeapFrogSpring.Input(\n            time_step = 0.01u\"s\",\n            time_end = 5.0u\"s\",\n            spring_constant = 50.0u\"N/m\",\n            initial_position = 1.0u\"m\",\n            mass = 1.0u\"kg\",\n        )\n\n        output = Common.run(Model{LeapFrogSpring}, input) |> collect\n        energy = map(output) do s\n            # correction for overshooting\n            LeapFrogSpring.drift!(input, s; fraction=-0.5)\n            LeapFrogSpring.energy(input, s)\n        end\n        fig = plot_result(input, output, energy)\n        save(\"docs/src/fig/mixin-a-spring.svg\", fig)\n    end\nend\n\nScript.main()#| id: mixin-a-spring\n\nSo far, what we did could have been achieved with different techniques, like dispatch and trait types. The real reason why we use ModuleMixins is to handle the composition of data (i.e. struct types and their fields). What we've seen here, is that the way ModuleMixins solves that problem blends nicely with an almost object-oriented style of programming.\n\nWe've seen how we can use ModuleMixins to compose models from smaller components. The Time component could be reused for a different model, and we could use what we had (a forward Euler method) and extend it (to Leap-frog method), giving us both reusability and extensibility.\n\nWhere ModuleMixins is absolutely needed, is when our problem grows in complexity, such that data composition is no longer trivially solved by ordinary object composition. The minimal example of such a problem occurs when we have a diamond shaped dependency tree.","title":"Using a better integrator"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Diamond-dependencies","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"There is a case where using a construct similar to ModuleMixins becomes inevitable: the diamond dependency pattern.\n\nusing ModuleMixins: @compose\n\n@compose module A\n    struct S a::Int end\nend\n\n@compose module B\n    @mixin A\n    struct S b::Int end\nend\n\n@compose module C\n    @mixin A\n    struct S c::Int end\nend\n\n@compose module D\n    @mixin B, C\n    struct S d::Int end\nend\n\nfieldnames(D.S)\n(:a, :b, :c, :d)\n\nWe may draw a dependency diagram for this example:\n\ngraph TD\n    A(\"A: a\")\n    B(\"B: a b\")\n    C(\"C: a c\")\n    D(\"D: a b c d\")\n\n    A --> B --> D\n    A --> C --> D","title":"Diamond dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#What-is-special-about-the-diamond?","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"If we had used composition to create these dependencies, we would have arrived at a different conclusion:\n\ngraph TD\n    A(\"A: a\")\n    B(\"B: (a) b\")\n    C(\"C: (a) c\")\n    D(\"D: ((a) b) ((a) c) d\")\n\n    A --> B --> D\n    A --> C --> D\n\nWe end up with two copies of A. We could implement this so that D.b.a and D.c.a point to the same object, but doing so increases the amount of indirection and complexity in our code needlessly.","title":"What is special about the diamond?"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Performance","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"#| file: examples/spring-benchmark.jl\nusing BenchmarkTools\nusing ModuleMixins: @compose\n\n<<mixin-a-spring>>\n\nmodule Compute\n    using ..Common: Model\n    using ..LeapFrogSpring: Input, State\n    using Unitful\n\n    function plain(input::Input)\n        state = State(time = 0.0u\"s\", position = input.initial_position, velocity = 0.0u\"m/s\")\n        n_steps = input.time_end / input.time_step |> Int\n\n        for i = 1:n_steps\n            a = -state.position * input.spring_constant / input.mass\n            state.velocity += a * input.time_step\n            state.time += 0.5*input.time_step\n            state.position += state.velocity * input.time_step\n            state.time += 0.5*input.time_step\n        end\n\n        return state\n    end\n\n    function fancy(::Type{Model{M}}, input::Input) where M\n        state = M.init(input)\n        n_steps = input.time_end / input.time_step |> Int\n\n        for i = 1:n_steps\n            M.step!(input, state)\n        end\n\n        return state\n    end\nend\n\nmodule Benchmark\n    using ..LeapFrogSpring\n    using Unitful\n    using ..Common: Model\n    using ..Compute\n    using BenchmarkTools\n\n    const input = LeapFrogSpring.Input(\n        time_step = 0.01u\"s\",\n        time_end = 5.0u\"s\",\n        spring_constant = 50.0u\"N/m\",\n        initial_position = 1.0u\"m\",\n        mass = 1.0u\"kg\",\n    )\n\n    function main()\n        a = @benchmark Compute.plain(Benchmark.input)\n        display(a)\n        b = @benchmark Compute.fancy(Model{LeapFrogSpring}, Benchmark.input)\n        display(b)\n    end\nend\n\nBenchmark.main()","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"30-blog/#Resources","page":"Objects, Inheritance and Modules in Julia","text":"Matthijs Cox's blog post on fruity design patterns\nEmergent Features of JuliaLang, part II: traits\n\n","title":"Resources"},{"category":"section","location":"40-studies/#Etudes-in-Macro-Programming","page":"Etudes in Macro Programming","text":"The way ModuleMixins is implemented, is that we start out with something relatively simple, and build out from that. This means there will be some redudant code. Macros are hard to engineer, this takes you through the entire process.\n\nBefore we explain the full @compose macro, we can do some finger exercises to understand the mechanisms behind its implementation.","title":"Etudes in Macro Programming"},{"category":"section","location":"40-studies/#@spec","page":"Etudes in Macro Programming","text":"The @spec macro creates a new module, and stores its own AST inside that module.\n\ndetails: src/Spec.jl\n#| file: src/Spec.jl\nmodule Spec\n\nusing MacroTools: @capture\nimport ..Common: strip_syntax_version\nexport @spec\n\n<<spec>>\n\nend\n\nWe may test that this works using a small example.\n\ndetails: test/SpecSpec.jl\n#| file: test/SpecSpec.jl\n<<test-spec-toplevel>>\n\n@testset \"ModuleMixins.Spec\" begin\n    using MacroTools: prewalk, rmlines\n    clean(expr) = prewalk(rmlines, expr)\n\n    <<test-spec>>\nend\n\n#| id: test-spec-toplevel\nusing ModuleMixins.Spec: @spec, @spec_mixin, @mixin\n\n@spec module MySpec\nconst msg = \"hello\"\nend\n\n#| id: test-spec\n@testset \"@spec\" begin\n    @test clean.(MySpec.AST) == clean.([:(const msg = \"hello\")])\n    @test MySpec.msg == \"hello\"\nend\n\nThis may seem like a silly example, but storing the AST of a module inside itself is very powerful. It means that inside macros we can always return to original expressions of other modules and devise ways of combining, composing and compiling new modules from them.\n\n#| id: spec\n\"\"\"\n    @spec module *name*\n        *body*...\n    end\n\nCreate a spec. The `@spec` macro itself doesn't perform any operations other than creating a module and storing its own AST as `const *name*.AST`.\n\nThis macro is only here for teaching purposes.\n\"\"\"\nmacro spec(mod)\n    @assert @capture(strip_syntax_version(mod), module name_\n    body__\n    end)\n\n    esc(Expr(:toplevel, :(module $name\n    $(body...)\n    const AST = $body\n    end)))\nend\n\nInside ModuleMixins we make extensive use of MacroTools.@capture. Preceding @capture with @assert is a quickfire way of making sure that our macro was called with the correct syntax.\n\nWhen defining a new module we have to create a top-level expression, and call esc on the entire expression to make sure no symbols are mangled.","title":"@spec"},{"category":"section","location":"40-studies/#@spec_mixin","page":"Etudes in Macro Programming","text":"We now add the @mixin syntax. This still doesn't do anything, other than storing the names of parent modules.\n\n#| id: test-spec-toplevel\n@spec_mixin module MyMixinSpecOne\n@mixin A\nend\n@spec_mixin module MyMixinSpecMany\n@mixin A, B, C\nend\n\n#| id: test-spec\n@testset \"@spec_mixin\" begin\n    @test MyMixinSpecOne.PARENTS == [:A]\n    @test MyMixinSpecMany.PARENTS == [:A, :B, :C]\nend\n\nHere's the @mixin macro:\n\n#| id: spec\nmacro mixin(deps)\n    if @capture(deps, (multiple_deps__,))\n        esc(:(const PARENTS = [$(QuoteNode.(multiple_deps)...)]))\n    else\n        esc(:(const PARENTS = [$(QuoteNode(deps))]))\n    end\nend\n\nThe QuoteNode calls prevent the symbols from being evaluated at macro expansion time. We need to make sure that the @mixin syntax is also available from within the module.\n\n#| id: spec\n\nmacro spec_mixin(mod)\n    @assert @capture(strip_syntax_version(mod), module name_\n    body__\n    end)\n\n    esc(Expr(:toplevel, :(module $name\n    import ..@mixin\n\n    $(body...)\n\n    const AST = $body\n    end)))\nend","title":"@spec_mixin"},{"category":"section","location":"40-studies/#Module-Templates","page":"Etudes in Macro Programming","text":"How cool it would be, if we can parametrise a module. Julia rules that modules can only be defined at top-level. So we have to go by the macro route once more. First, we try to implement the desired effect in isolation.\n\nEvery module template will have a name, body and a list of parameters. Each parameter may have a default argument specified, similar to how functions in Julia can have defaults.","title":"Module Templates"},{"category":"section","location":"40-studies/#Spec","page":"Etudes in Macro Programming","text":"#| file: test/TemplatesSpec.jl\nusing ModuleMixins.Templates: @lambda, @instantiate, ModuleTemplate\n\n@lambda module TempA{T}\n    struct S\n        x::T\n    end\nend\n\n@lambda module TempB{M}\n    const X = M.X\nend\n\nmodule M1\n    const X = 42\nend\n\n@testset \"ModuleMixins.Templates\" begin\n    @test TempA isa ModuleTemplate\n    @instantiate TempAi = TempA{Int}\n\n    @test TempAi.T === Int\n    @test TempAi.S(4).x isa Int\n\n    @instantiate TempAf = TempA{Float64}\n\n    @test TempAf.T === Float64\n    @test TempAf.S(4).x isa Float64\n\n    @instantiate TempBM1 = TempB{M1}\n    @test TempBM1.X == 42\nend","title":"Spec"},{"category":"section","location":"40-studies/#Implementation","page":"Etudes in Macro Programming","text":"#| id: template-parameter\nstruct Parameter\n    name::Symbol\n    default::Union{Some{Any}, Nothing}\nend\n\nname(parameter) = parameter.name\nhas_default(parameter) = parameter.default !== nothing\ndefault(parameter) = something(parameter.default)\n\nfunction make_parameter(expr)\n    if @capture(expr, name_ = default_)\n        return Parameter(name, Some(default))\n    elseif expr isa Symbol\n        return Parameter(expr, nothing)\n    else\n        error(\"Unknown parameter syntax `$(expr)`.\")\n    end\nend\n\nWhen arguments are passed to the template instantiation, they are either given positional (i.e. value only) or keyword style (key = value). When we convert a given syntax into an argument, we need to evaluate the value in the calling context.\n\n#| id: template-argument\nstruct Argument\n    name::Union{Symbol, Nothing}\n    value::Any\nend\n\npositional(argument) = argument.name === nothing\n\neval_argument(mod) = function (expr)\n    value = if @capture(expr, name_ = value_)\n        value\n    else\n        expr\n    end\n    return Argument(name, @eval(mod, $(value)))\nend\n\nGiven a list of parameters and a list of arguments, we can create a list of bound variables. Here we lay down some rules:\n\npositional arguments can never follow a keyword argument.\nat the end of the binding process, we obtain a name / value pair for each parameter, in the same order as the parameters were defined.\n\nA bound variable will result in the injection of a const definition.\n\n#| id: template-bound-variable\nstruct BoundVariable\n    name::Symbol\n    value::Any\nend\n\nfunction bind(pars, args)\n    positional_args = Iterators.takewhile(positional, args) |> collect\n    keyword_args = Dict(arg.name => arg for arg in args[length(positional_args)+1:end])\n    keyword_pars = pars[length(positional_args)+1:end]\n    @assert(!any(positional, values(keyword_args)))\n    positional_bindings = (BoundVariable(par.name, arg.value)\n        for (par, arg) in Iterators.zip(pars, positional_args))\n    keyword_bindings = (BoundVariable(par.name, par.name in keys(keyword_args) ?\n        keyword_args[par.name].value :\n        par.default) for par in keyword_pars)\n    bindings = Iterators.flatten(\n        (positional_bindings, keyword_bindings)) |> collect\n    @assert(name.(pars) == name.(bindings))\n    return bindings\nend\n\nfunction to_expression(bv::BoundVariable)\n    return :(const $(bv.name) = $(bv.value))\nend\n\nWe define a module with arguments using the @lambda macro, and create an instance with @instantiate.\n\n#| file: src/Templates.jl\nmodule Templates\n    using MacroTools: @capture\n    import ..Common: to_expression, strip_syntax_version\n    export @lambda, @instantiate\n\n    <<template-parameter>>\n    <<template-argument>>\n    <<template-bound-variable>>\n\n    struct ModuleTemplate\n        name::Symbol\n        environment::Module\n        parameters::Vector{Parameter}\n        body::Vector{Any}\n    end\n\n    macro lambda(expr)\n        @assert @capture(strip_syntax_version(expr), module name_ body__ end)\n        if isempty(body) | !@capture(body[1], {raw_parameters__})\n            return esc(Expr(:toplevel, :(module $name\n                $(body...)\n                const AST = $body\n            end)))\n        end\n        parameters = raw_parameters .|> make_parameter |> filter(!isnothing) |> collect\n        template = ModuleTemplate(name, __module__, parameters, body[2:end])\n        return esc(Expr(:toplevel, :(const $name = $template)))\n    end\n\n    macro instantiate(expr)\n        @assert @capture(expr, instance_name_ = template_name_{raw_arguments__})\n        arguments = raw_arguments .|> eval_argument(__module__)\n        template = @eval(__module__, $(template_name))\n        bound_vars = bind(template.parameters, arguments)\n        module_expr = Expr(:toplevel, :(module $(instance_name)\n            $(to_expression.(bound_vars)...)\n            $(template.body...)\n        end))\n        result = @eval(template.environment, $(module_expr))\n        return :(using $(nameof(template.environment)).$(instance_name))\n    end\nend\n\n","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#dev_docs","page":"Developer documentation","text":"note: Contributing guidelines\nIf you haven't, please read the Contributing guidelines first.\n\nIf you want to make contributions to this package that involves code, then this guide is for you.","title":"Developer documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#First-time-clone","page":"Developer documentation","text":"tip: If you have writing rights\nIf you have writing rights, you don't have to fork. Instead, simply clone and skip ahead. Whenever upstream is mentioned, use origin instead.\n\nIf this is the first time you work with this repository, follow the instructions below to clone the repository.\n\nFork this repo\nClone your repo (this will create a git remote called origin)\nAdd this repo as a remote:\ngit remote add upstream https://github.com/jhidding/ModuleMixins.jl\n\nThis will ensure that you have two remotes in your git: origin and upstream. You will create branches and push to origin, and you will fetch and update your local main branch from upstream.","title":"First time clone"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Linting-and-formatting","page":"Developer documentation","text":"Install a plugin on your editor to use EditorConfig. This will ensure that your editor is configured with important formatting settings.\n\nWe use https://pre-commit.com to run the linters and formatters. In particular, the Julia code is formatted using JuliaFormatter.jl, so please install it globally first:\n\njulia> # Press ]\npkg> activate\npkg> add JuliaFormatter\n\nTo install pre-commit, we recommend using pipx as follows:\n\n# Install pipx following the link\npipx install pre-commit\n\nWith pre-commit installed, activate it as a pre-commit hook:\n\npre-commit install\n\nTo run the linting and formatting manually, enter the command below:\n\npre-commit run -a\n\nNow, you can only commit if all the pre-commit tests pass.","title":"Linting and formatting"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Testing","page":"Developer documentation","text":"As with most Julia packages, you can just open Julia in the repository folder, activate the environment, and run test:\n\njulia> # press ]\npkg> activate .\npkg> test","title":"Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Working-on-a-new-issue","page":"Developer documentation","text":"We try to keep a linear history in this repo, so it is important to keep your branches up-to-date.\n\nFetch from the remote and fast-forward your local main\ngit fetch upstream\ngit switch main\ngit merge --ff-only upstream/main\nBranch from main to address the issue (see below for naming)\ngit switch -c 42-add-answer-universe\nPush the new local branch to your personal remote repository\ngit push -u origin 42-add-answer-universe\nCreate a pull request to merge your remote branch into the org main.","title":"Working on a new issue"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Branch-naming","page":"Developer documentation","text":"If there is an associated issue, add the issue number.\nIf there is no associated issue, and the changes are small, add a prefix such as \"typo\", \"hotfix\", \"small-refactor\", according to the type of update.\nIf the changes are not small and there is no associated issue, then create the issue first, so we can properly discuss the changes.\nUse dash separated imperative wording related to the issue (e.g., 14-add-tests, 15-fix-model, 16-remove-obsolete-files).","title":"Branch naming"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Commit-message","page":"Developer documentation","text":"Use imperative or present tense, for instance: Add feature or Fix bug.\nHave informative titles.\nWhen necessary, add a body with details.\nIf there are breaking changes, add the information to the commit message.","title":"Commit message"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Before-creating-a-pull-request","page":"Developer documentation","text":"tip: Atomic git commits\nTry to create \"atomic git commits\" (recommended reading: The Utopic Git History).\n\nMake sure the tests pass.\nMake sure the pre-commit tests pass.\nFetch any main updates from upstream and rebase your branch, if necessary:\ngit fetch upstream\ngit rebase upstream/main BRANCH_NAME\nThen you can open a pull request and work with the reviewer to address any issues.","title":"Before creating a pull request"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Building-and-viewing-the-documentation-locally","page":"Developer documentation","text":"Following the latest suggestions, we recommend using LiveServer to build the documentation. Here is how you do it:\n\nRun julia --project=docs to open Julia in the environment of the docs.\nIf this is the first time building the docs\nPress ] to enter pkg mode\nRun pkg> dev . to use the development version of your package\nPress backspace to leave pkg mode\nRun julia> using LiveServer\nRun julia> servedocs()","title":"Building and viewing the documentation locally"},{"category":"section","location":"91-developer/#Making-a-new-release","page":"Developer documentation","text":"To create a new release, you can follow these simple steps:\n\nCreate a branch release-x.y.z\nUpdate version in Project.toml\nUpdate the CHANGELOG.md:\nRename the section \"Unreleased\" to \"[x.y.z] - yyyy-mm-dd\" (i.e., version under brackets, dash, and date in ISO format)\nAdd a new section on top of it named \"Unreleased\"\nAdd a new link in the bottom for version \"x.y.z\"\nChange the \"[unreleased]\" link to use the latest version - end of line, vx.y.z ... HEAD.\nCreate a commit \"Release vx.y.z\", push, create a PR, wait for it to pass, merge the PR.\nGo back to main screen and click on the latest commit (link: https://github.com/jhidding/ModuleMixins.jl/commit/main)\nAt the bottom, write @JuliaRegistrator register\n\nAfter that, you only need to wait and verify:\n\nWait for the bot to comment (should take < 1m) with a link to a RP to the registry\nFollow the link and wait for a comment on the auto-merge\nThe comment should said all is well and auto-merge should occur shortly\nAfter the merge happens, TagBot will trigger and create a new GitHub tag. Check on https://github.com/jhidding/ModuleMixins.jl/releases\nAfter the release is create, a \"docs\" GitHub action will start for the tag.\nAfter it passes, a deploy action will run.\nAfter that runs, the stable docs should be updated. Check them and look for the version number.\n\n","title":"Making a new release"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#Contents","page":"Reference","text":"Pages = [\"95-reference.md\"]","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"95-reference/#Index","page":"Reference","text":"Pages = [\"95-reference.md\"]\n\n","title":"Index"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.substitute_top_level-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"substitute_top_level(var, val, mod, expr)\n\nTakes a syntax object expr and substitutes every occurence of module var for val, only if the resulting object is actually present in module mod. The mod module should correspond with a lookup of val in the caller's namespace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.substitute_top_level"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.@compose-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"@compose module Name\n    [@mixin Parents, ...]\n    ...\nend\n\nCreates a new composable module Name. Structs inside this module are merged with those of the same name in Parents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.@compose"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.@for_each-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"@for_each(M -> M.method(), lst::Vector{Symbol})\n\nCalls method() for each module in lst that actually implements that method. Here lst should be a vector of symbols that are all in the current module's namespace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.@for_each"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Passes.pass-Tuple{ModuleMixins.Passes.CompositePass, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"pass(x::CompositePass, expr)\n\nTries all passes in a composite pass in order, and returns with the first that succeeds (i.e. doesn't return no_match). You may create a CompositePass by adding passes with the + operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Passes.pass"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Passes.pass-Tuple{ModuleMixins.Passes.Pass, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"pass(x::Pass, expr)\n\nInterface. An implementation of the pass function should take a Pass object and an expression (or symbol), and return no_match if the expression did not match the pattern.\n\nYou can use the given Pass object to store information about this pass, return syntax that should replace the current expression, or nothing if it should be removed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Passes.pass"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Passes.walk-Tuple{ModuleMixins.Passes.Pass, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"walk(x::Pass, expr_list)\n\nCalls MacroTools.prewalk with the given Pass. If no_match is returned, the expression stays untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Passes.walk"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Spec.@spec-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"@spec module *name*\n    *body*...\nend\n\nCreate a spec. The @spec macro itself doesn't perform any operations other than creating a module and storing its own AST as const *name*.AST.\n\nThis macro is only here for teaching purposes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Spec.@spec"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Common.eval_to_expression-Tuple{Module, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"eval_to_expression(mod::Module, obj)\n\nGeneric function for converting syntax data objects to expressions, where parts of expressions need to be evaluated in a given module.\n\nDefaults to calling to_expression, dropping the mod argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Common.eval_to_expression"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Common.eval_to_expression-Tuple{Module}","page":"Reference","text":"eval_to_expression(mod::Module)\n\nCurrying version of eval_to_expression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Common.eval_to_expression"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Common.name-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"Get name attribute from syntax data object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Common.name"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Common.strip_syntax_version-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"strip_syntax_version(expr)\n\nFrom Julia 1.14, a module expression may carry the syntax version of the code it was parsed from as its first argument: Expr(:module, v\"1.14.0\", true, name, body). Whether it does depends on the project the code lives in, not on the Julia version alone. Remove the version, so that @capture patterns of the form module name_ body__ end match either way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Common.strip_syntax_version"},{"category":"function","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Common.to_expression","page":"Reference","text":"Generic function for converting syntax data objects to expressions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Common.to_expression"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#Core.Type-Union{Tuple{}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}} where {T<:Tuple, R<:Tuple}","page":"Reference","text":"Composite{T, R}(; kwargs...)\n\nConstruct a composite from flat keyword arguments. Each keyword goes to the part that has a field of that name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Core.Type"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.Composite","page":"Reference","text":"Composite{T <: Tuple, R <: Tuple}\n\nA value composed of parts, one per component. Properties are forwarded to the part that has a field of that name, so c.x works for any field x of any part. R is a tuple of RoleMap markers, recording which part fills each role.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.Composite"},{"category":"type","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.RoleMap","page":"Reference","text":"RoleMap{R, i}\n\nMarker type: role R is filled by part i of a composite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.RoleMap"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.closure-Tuple{Vararg{Module}}","page":"Reference","text":"closure(roots::Module...)\n\nCompute all components needed by roots, in dependency order (dependencies first), each component appearing once. Roles are resolved to a component implementing them: one of the roots if any, otherwise default_implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.closure"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.component-Union{Tuple{M}, Tuple{P}, Tuple{ModuleMixins.Procedural.Composite{P}, Val{M}}} where {P, M}","page":"Reference","text":"component(c::Composite, ::Val{M})\ncomponent(c::Composite, M::Module)\ncomponent(c::Composite, T::Type)\n\nThe part of c contributed by component M, the part of type T, or, if T is a role (an abstract type), the part contributed by the component that implements T. Pass a module as Val(M) for a type-stable lookup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.component"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.default_implementation-Tuple{Type}","page":"Reference","text":"default_implementation(R::Type)\n\nThe component (module) used to fill role R if no component implementing R is selected explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.default_implementation"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.implements-Tuple{Val, Type}","page":"Reference","text":"implements(::Val{M}, R::Type)\n\nWhether component M fills role R. Defaults to false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.implements"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.map_components-Tuple{Any, ModuleMixins.Procedural.Composite}","page":"Reference","text":"map_components(f, c::Composite)\n\nApply f to every part of c and compose the results that aren't nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.map_components"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.record_part-Tuple{Module, Symbol}","page":"Reference","text":"record_part(M::Module, name::Symbol)\n\nThe part that component M contributes to record name: the type M.name if M defines it, otherwise nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.record_part"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.record_type-Tuple{Symbol, Vararg{Module}}","page":"Reference","text":"record_type(name::Symbol, roots::Module...; parameters...)\n\nThe Composite type of record name, for the closure of roots. It has one part for every component that defines a type name, in dependency order.\n\nA type parameter of a part that is named after a record is filled with the composite type of that record. Any other type parameter is taken from parameters, by name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.record_type"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.requires-Tuple{Val}","page":"Reference","text":"requires(::Val{M})\n\nThe components (modules) or roles (abstract types) that component M depends on. Extend this for every component that has dependencies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.requires"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.resolve-Tuple{Module, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"resolve(R, roots)\n\nThe component that fills requirement R. A module is a component and resolves to itself. A role resolves to the one component in roots that implements it, or else to its default_implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.resolve"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Procedural.type_parameter_names-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"type_parameter_names(T)\n\nThe names of the type parameters of a UnionAll type, in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Procedural.type_parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.check_declaration-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"check_declaration(x, valid, declaration, name)\n\nCheck that x, named name in declaration, is an instance of valid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.check_declaration"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.declaration-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"declaration(expr)\n\nIf expr is one of the declarations @mixin, @requires or @implements, return the declaration name and its arguments. Otherwise return nothing. Arguments may be separated by spaces or by commas.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.declaration"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.field_types-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"field_types(struct_expr)\n\nThe declared types of the fields of a struct.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.field_types"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.map_struct-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"map_struct(f, expr)\n\nApply f to the struct definition in expr, looking through macro calls like @kwdef and docstrings. Returns expr unchanged if it doesn't define a struct.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.map_struct"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.mentioned-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"mentioned(names, expr)\n\nThe elements of names that occur as a symbol anywhere in expr, in the order of names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.mentioned"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.parameterize-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"parameterize(expr, struct_names)\n\nRewrite the struct defined in expr (if any), so that every other struct in struct_names that is mentioned in a field type becomes a type parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.parameterize"},{"category":"method","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.struct_name-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"struct_name(expr)\n\nThe name of the struct defined by expr, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.struct_name"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.@component-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"@component module Name\n    [@mixin Components...]\n    [@requires Requirements...]\n    [@implements Roles...]\n    ...\nend\n\nDefine a component module. The declarations expand into methods of ModuleMixins.Procedural:\n\n@mixin A, B: import A and B from the parent module (if they are plain names), and require them as components.\n@requires R: require role or component R.\n@implements R: the component implements role R.\n\nIf a field type of a struct mentions another struct S of the same module, the struct gets a type parameter S, which record_type fills with the composite of all parts of record S.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.@component"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.@implements-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"@implements Roles...\n\nDeclaration inside a @component module. See @component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.@implements"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.@mixin-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"@mixin Components...\n\nDeclaration inside a @component module. See @component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.@mixin"},{"category":"macro","location":"95-reference/#ModuleMixins.Syntactic.@requires-Tuple","page":"Reference","text":"@requires Requirements...\n\nDeclaration inside a @component module. See @component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModuleMixins.Syntactic.@requires"},{"category":"section","location":"45-passes/#Macro-passes","page":"Macro passes","text":"Our Big Friendly Macro is implemented in a series of passes. Each pass looks for items in the module that are transformed into different syntax, like structs, mixins and constructors. These passes are made composable so that they run in a single loop.\n\nWe use the prewalk function (from MacroTools.jl) to transform expressions and collect information into searchable data structures. We make a little abstraction over the prewalk function, so we can compose multiple transformations in a single tree walk.\n\nAn implementation of the pass function should take a Pass object and an expression (or symbol), and return no_match if the expression did not match the pattern.\n\nTypes that derive from Pass can be added into a composite CompositePass using the + operator.\n\n#| file: src/Passes.jl\nmodule Passes\n\nusing MacroTools: prewalk\n\nexport Pass, pass, no_match, walk\n\nabstract type Pass end\n\nstruct NoMatch end\n\nconst no_match = NoMatch()\n\n\"\"\"\n    pass(x::Pass, expr)\n\nInterface. An implementation of the `pass` function should take a `Pass` object\nand an expression (or symbol), and return `no_match` if the expression did not\nmatch the pattern.\n\nYou can use the given `Pass` object to store information about this pass, return\nsyntax that should replace the current expression, or `nothing` if it should be\nremoved.\n\"\"\"\nfunction pass(x::Pass, expr)\n    error(\"Can't call `pass` on abstract `Pass`.\")\nend\n\nstruct CompositePass <: Pass\n    parts::Vector{Pass}\nend\n\nBase.:+(a::CompositePass...) = CompositePass(splat(vcat)(getfield.(a, :parts)))\nBase.convert(::Type{CompositePass}, a::Pass) = CompositePass([a])\nBase.:+(a::Pass...) = splat(+)(convert.(CompositePass, a))\n\n\"\"\"\n    pass(x::CompositePass, expr)\n\nTries all passes in a composite pass in order, and returns with the first\nthat succeeds (i.e. doesn't return `no_match`). You may create a `CompositePass`\nby adding passes with the `+` operator.\n\"\"\"\nfunction pass(cp::CompositePass, expr)\n    for p in cp.parts\n        result = pass(p, expr)\n        if result !== no_match\n            return result\n        end\n    end\n    return no_match\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    walk(x::Pass, expr_list)\n\nCalls `MacroTools.prewalk` with the given `Pass`. If `no_match` is returned,\nthe expression stays untouched.\n\"\"\"\nfunction walk(x::Pass, expr_list)\n    function patch(expr)\n        result = pass(x, expr)\n        result === no_match ? expr : result\n    end\n    prewalk.(patch, expr_list)\nend\n\nend\n\nA composite pass tries all of its parts in order, returning the value of the first pass that doesn't return no_match.","title":"Macro passes"},{"category":"section","location":"45-passes/#Tests","page":"Macro passes","text":"details: test/PassesSpec.jl\n#| file: test/PassesSpec.jl\n@testset \"ModuleMixins.Passes\" begin\n    using ModuleMixins.Passes: Passes, Pass, no_match, pass, walk\n\n    <<test-passes>>\nend\n\nWe define a small pass that replaces some symbol with blip!.\n\n#| id: test-passes\nstruct BlipPass <: Pass\n    tag::Symbol\nend\n\nPasses.pass(p::BlipPass, expr) = expr == p.tag ? :blip! : no_match\n\nWe can test that this works on small tuple expression.\n\n#| id: test-passes\n@testset \"pass replacement\" begin\n    @test walk(BlipPass(:a), [:(a, b)])[1] == :(blip!, b)\n    @test walk(BlipPass(:b), [:(a, b)])[1] == :(a, blip!)\nend\n\nAnd then that it composes to replace both elements in the tuple.\n\n#| id: test-passes\n@testset \"pass composition\" begin\n    a = BlipPass(:a) + BlipPass(:b)\n    @test walk(a, [:(a, b)])[1] == :(blip!, blip!)\nend\n\n","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"20-example/#Example","page":"Example","text":"It cannot be helped that this example will seem a bit contrived.\n\nWe're modelling the movement of a spring. It can be useful to put some common definitions in a separate module.\n\n#| file: examples/spring.jl\n\nusing ModuleMixins\nusing CairoMakie\nusing Unitful\n\nmodule Common\n    abstract type AbstractInput end\n    abstract type AbstractState end\n\n    function initial_state(input::AbstractInput)\n        error(\"Can't construct from AbstractInput\")\n    end\n\n    export AbstractInput, AbstractState, initial_state\nend\n\n<<example-time>>\n<<example-spring>>\n<<example-run>>\n\nThe model has input parameters and a mutable state. We'll have a time component:\n\n#| id: example-time\n\n@compose module Time\n    using Unitful\n    using ..Common\n\n    @kwdef struct Input <: AbstractInput\n        t_step::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n        t_end::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    end\n\n    mutable struct State <: AbstractState\n        time::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    end\n\n    @constructor initial_state(input)::State[time] = (time = 0.0u\"s\",)\n\n    function step!(input::AbstractInput, state::AbstractState)\n        state.time += input.t_step\n    end\n\n    function run(model, input::AbstractInput)\n        s = model.initial_state(input)\n        Channel() do ch\n            while s.time < input.t_end\n                model.step!(input, s)\n                put!(ch, deepcopy(s))\n            end\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nNote that the run function is generic. And a component for the spring.\n\n#| id: example-spring\n\n@compose module Spring\n    @mixin Time\n    using ..Common\n    using Unitful\n\n    @kwdef struct Input <: AbstractInput\n        spring_constant::typeof(1.0u\"s^-2\")\n        initial_position::typeof(1.0u\"m\")\n    end\n\n    mutable struct State <: AbstractState\n        position::typeof(1.0u\"m\")\n        velocity::typeof(1.0u\"m/s\")\n    end\n\n    @constructor initial_state(input)::State[position, velocity] = (\n        position = input.initial_position,\n        velocity = 0.0u\"m/s\")\n\n    function step!(input::AbstractInput, state::AbstractState)\n        delta_v = -input.spring_constant * state.position\n        state.position += state.velocity * input.t_step\n        state.velocity += delta_v * input.t_step\n    end\nend\n\nNow we may compose these using @mixin:\n\n#| id: example-run\n\n@compose module Model\n    @mixin Time, Spring\n    using ..Common\n    using Unitful\n\n    function step!(input::Input, state::State)\n        Spring.step!(input, state)\n        Time.step!(input, state)\n    end\nend\n\nAnd see the result.\n\n#| id: example-run\n\nfunction plot_result()\n    input = Model.Input(\n        t_step = 0.001u\"s\",\n        t_end = 1.0u\"s\",\n        spring_constant = 250.0u\"s^-2\",\n        initial_position = 1.0u\"m\",\n    )\n\n    output = Time.run(Model, input) |> collect\n    times = [f.time for f in output]\n    pos = [f.position for f in output]\n\n    fig = Figure()\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, 1])\n    lines!(ax, times, pos)\n    save(\"docs/src/fig/plot.svg\", fig)\nend\n\nplot_result()\n\n(Image: Example output)\n\n","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Composition-by-closure","page":"Composition by closure","text":"note: Origin\nThis chapter, including the prototype code in src/Components/Procedural.jl and examples/composition, was written by Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic) at the request of Johan Hidding, on 2026-11-03 and 2026-11-04.\n\nThis page is a stand-alone prototype of the design discussed in issue #14. It doesn't use @compose at all. The question it tries to answer: can we keep what @compose is good at (diamond inheritance and automatically including all transitive dependencies), while composing values out of parts that Julia can dispatch on, instead of merging structs by name?\n\nThe prototype has three ingredients:\n\nA component is a module. A component declares what it depends on by extending requires. A dependency is either another component (a module) or a role (an abstract type). A component fills a role by declaring it with implements. Both are traits on Val{M}, where M is the component module, so they can be declared anywhere.\nA closure computation walks those declarations, resolves roles, and returns every needed component exactly once, dependencies first. This is the part of @compose that handles the diamond.\nA record is composed of parts: every component that defines a struct of the record's name contributes that struct. A Composite holds one part per contributing component. Property access is forwarded to the part that owns the field, so code can keep writing input.t_step, but each part keeps its own type for dispatch.\n\nNo struct has a special place. By convention, the parameters of a model are in a record called Input, but a component can contribute to any number of records (Input, State, Facies, ...), and records can contain other records.\n\nNo macros are needed: the closure is computed by an ordinary function, and a record type is just a Composite of a tuple of part types. The next chapter adds an optional macro, @component, to write components more concisely.","title":"Composition by closure"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Implementation","page":"Composition by closure","text":"The library is the module ModuleMixins.Procedural.\n\ndetails: src/Components/Procedural.jl\n#| file: src/Components/Procedural.jl\nmodule Procedural\n\nexport requires, implements, default_implementation, closure,\n    record_part, record_type, Composite, parts, component, map_components\n\n<<composition-requires>>\n<<composition-closure>>\n<<composition-composite>>\n<<composition-component>>\n<<composition-records>>\n\nend","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Requirements-and-roles","page":"Composition by closure","text":"A component is identified by its module M. The traits dispatch on Val{M}, because methods can't dispatch on a module value directly. Inside a module, its own name is bound to the module, so a component declares its dependencies as requires(::Val{Spring}) = (Time,). For convenience, requires and implements can also be called with a module.\n\nBy default a component has no dependencies and fills no roles. Roles are abstract types, but they only serve as names: nothing needs to subtype them. Since implements is a trait, a component can fill any number of roles, and the declaration can be made anywhere, for example to adapt a component from another package. A role may name a default implementation, used when nothing else fills it.\n\n#| id: composition-requires\n\"\"\"\n    requires(::Val{M})\n\nThe components (modules) or roles (abstract types) that component `M`\ndepends on. Extend this for every component that has dependencies.\n\"\"\"\nrequires(::Val) = ()\nrequires(M::Module) = requires(Val(M))\n\n\"\"\"\n    implements(::Val{M}, R::Type)\n\nWhether component `M` fills role `R`. Defaults to `false`.\n\"\"\"\nimplements(::Val, ::Type) = false\nimplements(M::Module, R::Type) = implements(Val(M), R)\n\n\"\"\"\n    default_implementation(R::Type)\n\nThe component (module) used to fill role `R` if no component implementing `R`\nis selected explicitly.\n\"\"\"\ndefault_implementation(R::Type) =\n    error(\"no component implementing role $(R) was selected, and there is no default\")","title":"Requirements and roles"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Closure","page":"Composition by closure","text":"closure does a depth-first walk from the selected components, appending each component after its dependencies, so that every component appears once. Roles are resolved first: a component selected explicitly takes precedence over the default implementation. Selecting two implementations of the same role is an error, and so is a cycle.\n\n#| id: composition-closure\n\"\"\"\n    resolve(R, roots)\n\nThe component that fills requirement `R`. A module is a component and resolves\nto itself. A role resolves to the one component in `roots` that implements it,\nor else to its `default_implementation`.\n\"\"\"\nresolve(M::Module, roots) = M\n\nfunction resolve(R::Type, roots)\n    candidates = [M for M in roots if implements(M, R)]\n    length(candidates) > 1 &&\n        error(\"role $(R) is implemented by more than one component: $(candidates)\")\n    isempty(candidates) || return only(candidates)\n    return default_implementation(R)\nend\n\nresolve(x, roots) = error(\"requirement $(x) is neither a component (module) nor a role (type)\")\n\n\"\"\"\n    closure(roots::Module...)\n\nCompute all components needed by `roots`, in dependency order (dependencies\nfirst), each component appearing once. Roles are resolved to a component\nimplementing them: one of the `roots` if any, otherwise `default_implementation`.\n\"\"\"\nfunction closure(roots::Module...)\n    order = Module[]\n    visiting = Module[]\n\n    function visit(R)\n        M = resolve(R, roots)\n        M in order && return\n        M in visiting && error(\"cyclic requirement: $(join([visiting; M], \" -> \"))\")\n        push!(visiting, M)\n        foreach(visit, requires(M))\n        pop!(visiting)\n        push!(order, M)\n    end\n\n    foreach(visit, roots)\n    return Tuple(order)\nend","title":"Closure"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Composite-values","page":"Composition by closure","text":"A Composite wraps a tuple of parts. Property access is forwarded to the part that has a field of that name. The lookup is done in a generated function, so for a constant property name it resolves at compile time and input.t_step is as fast as a field access.\n\nThe second type parameter records which part fills each role that a component in the model requires. It is a tuple of RoleMap{R, i} markers, meaning \"role R is filled by part i\". This is needed because role lookup also has to happen at compile time, but the generator of a @generated function may not call implements: methods added after the generator is defined, like the implements declarations of components, aren't reliably visible to it. So roles are resolved by record_type, which runs as ordinary code, and the generator only reads the result from the type. A composite built directly from parts, like the state composites below, has no roles.\n\n#| id: composition-composite\n\"\"\"\n    RoleMap{R, i}\n\nMarker type: role `R` is filled by part `i` of a composite.\n\"\"\"\nstruct RoleMap{R, i} end\n\n\"\"\"\n    Composite{T <: Tuple, R <: Tuple}\n\nA value composed of parts, one per component. Properties are forwarded to the\npart that has a field of that name, so `c.x` works for any field `x` of any\npart. `R` is a tuple of `RoleMap` markers, recording which part fills each role.\n\"\"\"\nstruct Composite{T <: Tuple, R <: Tuple}\n    parts::T\nend\n\nComposite(parts::Tuple) = Composite{typeof(parts), Tuple{}}(parts)\n\nparts(c::Composite) = getfield(c, :parts)\n\n# Index of the part that owns field `name`. Computed at compile time.\n@generated function _owner(::Type{T}, ::Val{name}) where {T <: Tuple, name}\n    i = findfirst(P -> hasfield(P, name), fieldtypes(T))\n    i === nothing ? :(error(\"no component has a field `$(name)`\")) : i\nend\n\nBase.getproperty(c::Composite, name::Symbol) =\n    getfield(parts(c)[_owner(typeof(parts(c)), Val(name))], name)\n\nBase.setproperty!(c::Composite, name::Symbol, value) =\n    setproperty!(parts(c)[_owner(typeof(parts(c)), Val(name))], name, value)\n\nBase.propertynames(c::Composite) = Tuple(Iterators.flatten(fieldnames.(typeof.(parts(c)))))\n\nfunction Base.show(io::IO, c::Composite)\n    print(io, \"Composite(\")\n    join(io, parts(c), \", \")\n    print(io, \")\")\nend\n\nA component reaches its dependencies through component, which looks up a part by its component, by its type, or by the role its component implements. This is where dispatch comes in: the part that comes back has its own concrete type.\n\nA part belongs to the module that defines its type, so the lookup by component only needs parentmodule, which is part of Julia and can be called at compile time. For that, the module has to be known at compile time, as a type parameter: component(c, Val(Spring)). Unlike symbols, modules are mutable objects, and Julia doesn't propagate them as constants into the functions they are passed to. So component(c, Spring) gives the right part, but its type isn't inferred. Inside a method, the type of the part is usually known as well, and component(c, Spring.Input) is type-stable too.\n\nA part type is looked up by subtyping, so a parametric part like FaciesBase.Input is found without naming its type parameters. Roles are abstract types, and are looked up in the role map. Only a role that no component in the model requires falls back to calling implements at run time. That still gives the right part, but it isn't type-stable either.\n\n#| id: composition-component\n# Index of the part defined in module `M`. Computed at compile time.\n@generated function _component_index(::Type{P}, ::Val{M}) where {P <: Tuple, M}\n    i = findfirst(X -> parentmodule(X) === M, fieldtypes(P))\n    i === nothing ? :(error(\"component $(M) has no part in this record\")) : i\nend\n\n# Index of the part of type `T`, or that implements role `T`. Computed at\n# compile time, except for roles that are not in the role map.\n@generated function _type_index(::Type{P}, ::Type{R}, ::Type{T}) where {P <: Tuple, R <: Tuple, T}\n    if isabstracttype(T)\n        for m in fieldtypes(R)\n            m.parameters[1] === T && return m.parameters[2]\n        end\n        return :(_role_index_dynamic(P, T))\n    end\n    i = findfirst(X -> X <: T, fieldtypes(P))\n    i === nothing ? :(error(\"no part of type $(T) in this record\")) : i\nend\n\nfunction _role_index_dynamic(::Type{P}, ::Type{T}) where {P <: Tuple, T}\n    i = findfirst(X -> implements(parentmodule(X), T), fieldtypes(P))\n    i === nothing && error(\"no part implementing role $(T)\")\n    return i\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    component(c::Composite, ::Val{M})\n    component(c::Composite, M::Module)\n    component(c::Composite, T::Type)\n\nThe part of `c` contributed by component `M`, the part of type `T`, or, if `T`\nis a role (an abstract type), the part contributed by the component that\nimplements `T`. Pass a module as `Val(M)` for a type-stable lookup.\n\"\"\"\ncomponent(c::Composite{P}, ::Val{M}) where {P, M} = parts(c)[_component_index(P, Val(M))]\ncomponent(c::Composite, M::Module) = component(c, Val(M))\ncomponent(c::Composite{P, R}, ::Type{T}) where {P, R, T} = parts(c)[_type_index(P, R, T)]\n\n\"\"\"\n    map_components(f, c::Composite)\n\nApply `f` to every part of `c` and compose the results that aren't `nothing`.\n\"\"\"\nmap_components(f, c::Composite) = Composite(Tuple(filter(!isnothing, map(f, parts(c)))))","title":"Composite values"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Records","page":"Composition by closure","text":"A record is named by a symbol. Component M contributes to record name if it defines a type of that name: the binding M.name has to be a type whose parentmodule is M, so that a type imported from elsewhere doesn't count.\n\nRecords can contain other records. In CarboKitten, components contribute fields not only to the top-level input but also to each facies: Production, CellularAutomaton and Denudation each define a Facies struct. The part that holds the facies is then parametric, and its type parameter is named after the record it stands for:\n\n@kwdef struct Input{Facies}\n    facies::Vector{Facies} = []\nend\n\nInside Input, Facies now stands for the composite of all Facies parts in the model. record_type builds that composite and substitutes it for the parameter. A record that (indirectly) contains itself is an error.\n\nA part can also have type parameters that are not records. These are set by keyword arguments to record_type, and are matched by name. The typical use is storage: on a GPU, the arrays in a model's state have a different type than on the CPU, and a library like Adapt needs the field types to be parameters to convert between the two.\n\n@kwdef struct State{Storage <: AbstractMatrix{Float64}}\n    height::Storage\nend\n\nconst CPUState = record_type(:State, Model; Storage = Matrix{Float64})\n\nComponents that use the same parameter name share one setting, so a single keyword switches the storage of a whole model. Every type parameter must either name a record, or be given as a keyword, so that a record type is always concrete. A parameter that does both is ambiguous and also an error. Keywords that no part uses are ignored, so the same keywords can be passed for every record of a model.\n\nrecord_type computes the closure, resolves every role that a component in the model requires, and checks that no field name is defined by two parts of a record, since forwarding would be ambiguous otherwise.\n\n#| id: composition-records\n\"\"\"\n    record_part(M::Module, name::Symbol)\n\nThe part that component `M` contributes to record `name`: the type `M.name`\nif `M` defines it, otherwise `nothing`.\n\"\"\"\nfunction record_part(M::Module, name::Symbol)\n    isdefined(M, name) || return nothing\n    T = getfield(M, name)\n    T isa Type && parentmodule(T) === M ? T : nothing\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    type_parameter_names(T)\n\nThe names of the type parameters of a `UnionAll` type, in order.\n\"\"\"\nfunction type_parameter_names(T)\n    names = Symbol[]\n    while T isa UnionAll\n        push!(names, T.var.name)\n        T = T.body\n    end\n    return names\nend\n\nfunction check_unique_fields(types)\n    names = collect(Iterators.flatten(fieldnames.(types)))\n    duplicate = [n for n in unique(names) if count(==(n), names) > 1]\n    isempty(duplicate) || error(\"field names defined by more than one component: $(duplicate)\")\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    record_type(name::Symbol, roots::Module...; parameters...)\n\nThe `Composite` type of record `name`, for the closure of `roots`. It has one\npart for every component that defines a type `name`, in dependency order.\n\nA type parameter of a part that is named after a record is filled with the\ncomposite type of that record. Any other type parameter is taken from\n`parameters`, by name.\n\"\"\"\nfunction record_type(name::Symbol, roots::Module...; parameters...)\n    components = closure(roots...)\n    roles = unique(R for M in components for R in requires(M) if R isa Type)\n    resolved = [R => resolve(R, roots) for R in roles]\n    return _record_type(name, components, resolved, NamedTuple(parameters), Symbol[])\nend\n\nis_record(name, components) = any(M -> record_part(M, name) !== nothing, components)\n\nfunction _record_type(name, components, resolved, parameters, visiting)\n    name in visiting && error(\"record $(name) contains itself: $(join([visiting; name], \" -> \"))\")\n    owners = [M for M in components if record_part(M, name) !== nothing]\n    isempty(owners) && error(\"no component defines a record part `$(name)`\")\n\n    function parameter(P, n)\n        record = is_record(n, components)\n        given = haskey(parameters, n)\n        record && given &&\n            error(\"type parameter `$(n)` of $(P) names a record, and is also given as a keyword\")\n        record && return _record_type(n, components, resolved, parameters, visiting)\n        given && return parameters[n]\n        error(\"type parameter `$(n)` of $(P) is not a record, and is not given as a keyword to `record_type`\")\n    end\n\n    push!(visiting, name)\n    record_parts = map(owners) do M\n        P = record_part(M, name)\n        inner = type_parameter_names(P)\n        isempty(inner) ? P : P{map(n -> parameter(P, n), inner)...}\n    end\n    pop!(visiting)\n\n    check_unique_fields(record_parts)\n    rolemap = [RoleMap{R, findfirst(==(M), owners)} for (R, M) in resolved if M in owners]\n    return Composite{Tuple{record_parts...}, Tuple{rolemap...}}\nend\n\nUsers shouldn't need to know how a record is split into parts, so a composite is constructed from flat keyword arguments. Each argument is routed to the part that has a field of that name, and unknown arguments are an error. Contained records are passed as named tuples of flat arguments: converting a named tuple to a composite type calls its keyword constructor, so a field facies::Vector{Facies} accepts a vector of named tuples.\n\n#| id: composition-records\n\"\"\"\n    Composite{T, R}(; kwargs...)\n\nConstruct a composite from flat keyword arguments. Each keyword goes to the\npart that has a field of that name.\n\"\"\"\nfunction (::Type{Composite{T, R}})(; kwargs...) where {T <: Tuple, R <: Tuple}\n    types = fieldtypes(T)\n    known = Set(Iterators.flatten(fieldnames.(types)))\n    unknown = setdiff(keys(kwargs), known)\n    isempty(unknown) || error(\"unknown arguments: $(join(unknown, \", \"))\")\n    return Composite{T, R}(map(types) do P\n        P(; (k => v for (k, v) in kwargs if hasfield(P, k))...)\n    end)\nend\n\nBase.convert(::Type{C}, x::NamedTuple) where {C <: Composite} = C(; x...)","title":"Records"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Adapt","page":"Composition by closure","text":"Adapt converts the storage of nested structures, for instance to move a model state to the GPU, or to turn arrays into device arrays when they are passed to a kernel. A composite is adapted by adapting each of its parts. The result is again a composite, of a different type, but with the same role map. Each part has to support Adapt itself, typically by Adapt.@adapt_structure. A part that doesn't is returned unchanged, as Adapt does by default.\n\nThis method lives in a package extension, so ModuleMixins doesn't depend on Adapt.\n\n#| file: ext/ModuleMixinsAdaptExt.jl\nmodule ModuleMixinsAdaptExt\n\nusing Adapt: Adapt, adapt\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural: Composite, parts\n\nfunction Adapt.adapt_structure(to, c::Composite{T, R}) where {T, R}\n    adapted = map(p -> adapt(to, p), parts(c))\n    return Composite{typeof(adapted), R}(adapted)\nend\n\nend","title":"Adapt"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Tests","page":"Composition by closure","text":"The tests use components X, Y and Z. Y requires roles A and B, which X both fills, and Z is the default for A. Components Owner and Extra both contribute to the record Item, which Owner.Input contains.\n\n#| file: test/ProceduralSpec.jl\nmodule ProceduralSpec\nusing Test\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\nimport ModuleMixins.Procedural: requires, implements, default_implementation\n\nabstract type A end\nabstract type B end\nabstract type C end\n\nmodule X\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        x::Int = 1\n    end\nend\n\nmodule Y\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        y::Int = 2\n    end\nend\n\nmodule Z\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        z::Int = 3\n    end\nend\n\nmodule W\n    using ..X: Input\nend\n\nrequires(::Val{Y}) = (A, B)\nimplements(::Val{X}, ::Type{A}) = true\nimplements(::Val{X}, ::Type{B}) = true\nimplements(::Val{X}, ::Type{C}) = true\nimplements(::Val{Z}, ::Type{A}) = true\ndefault_implementation(::Type{A}) = Z\n\n@testset \"Procedural\" begin\n    @test implements(X, A)\n    @test implements(X, B)\n    @test !implements(Z, B)\n\n    @test closure(Y, X) == (X, Y)\n    @test_throws ErrorException closure(Y)  # no default for B\n    @test_throws ErrorException closure(Y, X, Z)  # A filled twice\n\n    @test record_part(X, :Input) === X.Input\n    @test record_part(X, :State) === nothing\n    @test record_part(W, :Input) === nothing  # imported, not defined\n\n    M = record_type(:Input, Y, X)\n    m = M(x = 10)\n    @test m.x == 10 && m.y == 2\n    @test component(m, A) === component(m, B) === component(m, X)\n    @test (@inferred component(m, B)) isa X.Input\n    @test (@inferred component(m, Val(Y))) isa Y.Input\n    @test (@inferred component(m, Y.Input)) isa Y.Input\n    @test component(m, Y) isa Y.Input\n\n    # C is not required by any component, so it is found at run time\n    @test component(m, C) isa X.Input\n\n    @test_throws ErrorException M(w = 1)\n    @test_throws ErrorException component(m, Z)\n    @test_throws ErrorException record_type(:State, Y, X)\nend\n\nmodule Owner\n    @kwdef struct Item\n        name::String = \"\"\n    end\n    @kwdef struct Input{Item}\n        items::Vector{Item} = []\n    end\nend\n\n# contributes to `Item`, but not to `Input`\nmodule Extra\n    @kwdef struct Item\n        weight::Float64 = 1.0\n    end\nend\n\nmodule Loop\n    struct Input{Input}\n        x::Input\n    end\nend\n\nrequires(::Val{Extra}) = (Owner,)\n\n@testset \"Procedural records\" begin\n    M = record_type(:Input, Extra)\n    m = M(items = [(name = \"a\", weight = 2.0), (name = \"b\",)])\n    @test m.items[1].name == \"a\" && m.items[1].weight == 2.0\n    @test m.items[2].weight == 1.0\n    @test component(m.items[1], Extra) isa Extra.Item\n    @test component(m, Owner) isa Owner.Input\n    @test component(m, Owner.Input) isa Owner.Input\n    @test_throws ErrorException component(m, Extra)\n    @test (@inferred (m -> m.items[1].weight)(m)) == 2.0\n\n    # without `Extra`, items have only the owner's part\n    @test record_type(:Input, Owner)(items = [(name = \"c\",)]).items[1].name == \"c\"\n    @test_throws ErrorException record_type(:Input, Owner)(items = [(weight = 1.0,)])\n\n    @test_throws ErrorException record_type(:Input, Loop)\nend\n\nComponents Grid and Growth both have a type parameter Storage, and Grid also contains a record. To test Adapt, we use an adaptor that converts Float64 vectors to Float32.\n\n#| file: test/ProceduralSpec.jl\nmodule Grid\n    @kwdef struct Cell\n        label::String = \"\"\n    end\n    @kwdef struct State{Cell, Storage <: AbstractVector{<:AbstractFloat}}\n        cells::Vector{Cell} = []\n        height::Storage\n    end\nend\n\nmodule Growth\n    @kwdef struct Cell\n        rate::Float64 = 0.0\n    end\n    @kwdef mutable struct State{Storage}\n        growth::Storage\n    end\nend\n\nrequires(::Val{Growth}) = (Grid,)\n\nmodule Clash\n    @kwdef struct State{Storage}\n        x::Storage\n    end\n    struct Storage end\nend\n\nusing Adapt\n\nAdapt.@adapt_structure Grid.State\nAdapt.@adapt_structure Growth.State\n\nstruct ToFloat32 end\nAdapt.adapt_storage(::ToFloat32, x::Vector{Float64}) = Float32.(x)\n\n@testset \"Procedural type parameters\" begin\n    S = record_type(:State, Growth; Storage = Vector{Float64}, Unused = Int)\n    @test isconcretetype(S)\n    s = S(cells = [(label = \"a\", rate = 1.0)], height = [1.0, 2.0], growth = [0.5, 0.5])\n    @test s.height isa Vector{Float64}\n    @test s.cells[1].rate == 1.0\n    @test (@inferred (s -> s.height[2])(s)) == 2.0\n\n    @test_throws ErrorException record_type(:State, Growth)  # Storage not given\n    @test_throws TypeError record_type(:State, Growth; Storage = Vector{Int})  # violates bound\n    @test_throws ErrorException record_type(:State, Clash; Storage = Int)  # record and keyword\n\n    t = adapt(ToFloat32(), s)\n    @test t.height isa Vector{Float32} && t.growth == Float32[0.5, 0.5]\n    @test t.cells == s.cells\n    @test component(t, Growth) isa Growth.State{Vector{Float32}}\nend\nend","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#An-elemental-example","page":"Composition by closure","text":"A small diamond: Double and Square both depend on a source of a value, and Sum depends on both. The source is a role, AbstractSource, with two implementations, Constant (the default) and Ramp. Every component has a value method that dispatches on the type of its own part.\n\nflowchart TD\n    Sum --> Double --> Source[AbstractSource]\n    Sum --> Square --> Source\n    Constant -. implements .-> Source\n    Ramp -. implements .-> Source\n\n#| file: examples/composition/elemental.jl\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\nimport ModuleMixins.Procedural: requires, implements, default_implementation\nusing Test\n\n<<elemental-components>>\n<<elemental-run>>\n\nThe role is an abstract type, and value is the function every component implements.\n\n#| id: elemental-components\nabstract type AbstractSource end\nfunction value end\n\nmodule Constant\n    import ..AbstractSource, ..value, ..implements\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        c::Float64 = 1.0\n    end\n    implements(::Val{Constant}, ::Type{AbstractSource}) = true\n    value(s::Input, model) = s.c\nend\n\nmodule Ramp\n    import ..AbstractSource, ..value, ..implements\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        slope::Float64 = 1.0\n        x::Float64 = 0.0\n    end\n    implements(::Val{Ramp}, ::Type{AbstractSource}) = true\n    value(s::Input, model) = s.slope * s.x\nend\n\ndefault_implementation(::Type{AbstractSource}) = Constant\n\nDouble and Square require the role, not a specific implementation, and find it with component.\n\n#| id: elemental-components\nmodule Double\n    import ..AbstractSource, ..value, ..requires\n    using ModuleMixins.Procedural: component\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        factor::Float64 = 2.0\n    end\n    requires(::Val{Double}) = (AbstractSource,)\n    value(d::Input, model) = d.factor * value(component(model, AbstractSource), model)\nend\n\nmodule Square\n    import ..AbstractSource, ..value, ..requires\n    using ModuleMixins.Procedural: component\n    struct Input end\n    requires(::Val{Square}) = (AbstractSource,)\n    value(::Input, model) = value(component(model, AbstractSource), model)^2\nend\n\nmodule Sum\n    import ..value, ..requires\n    using ModuleMixins.Procedural: component\n    import ..Double, ..Square\n    struct Input end\n    requires(::Val{Sum}) = (Double, Square)\n    value(::Input, model) =\n        value(component(model, Double.Input), model) +\n        value(component(model, Square.Input), model)\nend\n\nSelecting only Sum is enough: the closure includes the source once, even though two components require it.\n\n#| id: elemental-run\n@show closure(Sum)\n\nconst Model = record_type(:Input, Sum)\nm = Model(c = 3.0, factor = 10.0)\n@show m\n@show m.c m.factor\n@show value(component(m, Sum.Input), m)\n\nclosure(Sum) = (Main.Constant, Main.Double, Main.Square, Main.Sum)\nm = Composite(Main.Constant.Input(3.0), Main.Double.Input(10.0), Main.Square.Input(), Main.Sum.Input())\nm.c = 3.0\nm.factor = 10.0\nvalue(component(m, Sum.Input), m) = 39.0\n\nTo substitute the source, select Ramp explicitly. Both branches of the diamond now share the Ramp: 2 cdot 3 + 3^2 = 15.\n\n#| id: elemental-run\nconst RampModel = record_type(:Input, Sum, Ramp)\n@show closure(Sum, Ramp)\nr = RampModel(slope = 2.0, x = 1.5)\n@show value(component(r, Sum.Input), r)\n\nclosure(Sum, Ramp) = (Main.Ramp, Main.Double, Main.Square, Main.Sum)\nvalue(component(r, Sum.Input), r) = 15.0\n\nProperty access and the whole value computation are type-stable. Mistakes are caught when the model is built: an unknown argument, or two components filling the same role.\n\n#| id: elemental-run\nget_c(m) = m.c\nget_slope(m) = m.slope\n@inferred get_c(m)\n@inferred get_slope(r)\n@inferred value(component(r, Sum.Input), r)\n\n@test_throws ErrorException Model(colour = :red)\n@test_throws ErrorException record_type(:Input, Sum, Ramp, Constant)","title":"An elemental example"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#One-component,-two-roles","page":"Composition by closure","text":"A ramp also has a gradient. Suppose there is a second role, AbstractGradient, and a component Slope that reports the gradient of whatever fills that role. Ramp can fill this role as well. The declaration is made outside the Ramp module, as it would be if Ramp came from another package.\n\n#| id: elemental-run\nabstract type AbstractGradient end\nfunction gradient end\n\ngradient(s::Ramp.Input, model) = s.slope\nimplements(::Val{Ramp}, ::Type{AbstractGradient}) = true\n\nmodule Slope\n    import ..AbstractGradient, ..value, ..gradient, ..requires\n    using ModuleMixins.Procedural: component\n    struct Input end\n    requires(::Val{Slope}) = (AbstractGradient,)\n    value(::Input, model) = gradient(component(model, AbstractGradient), model)\nend\n\nSelecting Ramp once fills both roles, so Sum and Slope read from the same part. AbstractGradient has no default implementation, so a model that needs a gradient without selecting Ramp is an error.\n\n#| id: elemental-run\nconst BothModel = record_type(:Input, Sum, Slope, Ramp)\n@show closure(Sum, Slope, Ramp)\nb = BothModel(slope = 2.0, x = 1.5)\n@show value(component(b, Sum.Input), b)\n@show value(component(b, Slope.Input), b)\n@test component(b, AbstractSource) === component(b, AbstractGradient)\n\n@inferred value(component(b, Slope.Input), b)\n@test_throws ErrorException record_type(:Input, Sum, Slope)\nprintln(\"all checks passed\")\n\nclosure(Sum, Slope, Ramp) = (Main.Ramp, Main.Double, Main.Square, Main.Sum, Main.Slope)\nvalue(component(b, Sum.Input), b) = 15.0\nvalue(component(b, Slope.Input), b) = 2.0\nall checks passed","title":"One component, two roles"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Porting-the-spring-example","page":"Composition by closure","text":"Here is the spring example on top of Procedural. The structure stays the same: a Time component, a Spring component that depends on it, and a model. The differences:\n\nSpring declares its dependency with requires instead of @mixin.\ninitial_state and step! are generic functions that dispatch on the part (the first argument). The composite versions loop over the parts in dependency order, which replaces @constructor and the hand-written Model.step!.\nThe model is a single line, record_type(:Input, Spring).\n\n#| file: examples/composition/spring.jl\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\nimport ModuleMixins.Procedural: requires\nusing Unitful\nusing CairoMakie\n\n<<spring-common>>\n<<spring-time>>\n<<spring-spring>>\n<<spring-damping>>\n<<spring-run>>\n\nThe common module defines the two generic functions, with a no-op default for components that have no state or don't step.\n\n#| id: spring-common\nmodule Common\n    using ModuleMixins.Procedural: Composite, parts, map_components\n\n    \"\"\"\n        initial_state(part, input)\n\n    The initial state of a single component, or `nothing` if it has no state.\n    \"\"\"\n    initial_state(part, input) = nothing\n    initial_state(input::Composite) = map_components(p -> initial_state(p, input), input)\n\n    \"\"\"\n        step!(part, input, state)\n\n    Advance the state of a single component. Components step in dependency order.\n    \"\"\"\n    step!(part, input, state) = nothing\n    step!(input::Composite, state) = foreach(p -> step!(p, input, state), parts(input))\n\n    export initial_state, step!\nend\n\nEach component has its own Input and State types. Methods take the component's own part as first argument for dispatch, and the full composite input and state to reach other components' fields.\n\n#| id: spring-time\nmodule Time\n    using Unitful\n    import ..Common: initial_state, step!\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        t_step::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n        t_end::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    end\n\n    mutable struct State\n        time::typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    end\n\n    initial_state(::Input, input) = State(0.0u\"s\")\n    step!(::Input, input, state) = (state.time += input.t_step)\n\n    function run(input)\n        s = initial_state(input)\n        Channel() do ch\n            while s.time < input.t_end\n                step!(input, s)\n                put!(ch, deepcopy(s))\n            end\n        end\n    end\nend\n\n#| id: spring-spring\nmodule Spring\n    using Unitful\n    import ..Common: initial_state, step!\n    import ..requires\n    import ..Time\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        spring_constant::typeof(1.0u\"s^-2\")\n        initial_position::typeof(1.0u\"m\")\n    end\n\n    mutable struct State\n        position::typeof(1.0u\"m\")\n        velocity::typeof(1.0u\"m/s\")\n    end\n\n    requires(::Val{Spring}) = (Time,)\n\n    initial_state(::Input, input) = State(input.initial_position, 0.0u\"m/s\")\n\n    function step!(::Input, input, state)\n        delta_v = -input.spring_constant * state.position\n        state.position += state.velocity * input.t_step\n        state.velocity += delta_v * input.t_step\n    end\nend\n\nThe original Model.step! stepped the spring before the time. Here the time steps first, because it is a dependency. The spring doesn't read the time, so the result is the same: running both versions gives identical positions.","title":"Porting the spring example"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Extending-from-outside","page":"Composition by closure","text":"A new component doesn't need anything from the namespaces of the components it builds on, other than the names it uses. Damping is written as if it lived in a separate package, referring to everything by absolute path. Selecting Damping pulls in Spring, and through it Time.\n\n#| id: spring-damping\nmodule Damping\n    using Unitful\n    import ModuleMixins.Procedural: requires\n    import Main.Common: step!\n    import Main.Spring\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        damping::typeof(1.0u\"s^-1\")\n    end\n\n    requires(::Val{Damping}) = (Spring,)\n\n    step!(::Input, input, state) =\n        (state.velocity -= input.damping * state.velocity * input.t_step)\nend\n\n#| id: spring-run\nconst Model = record_type(:Input, Spring)\nconst DampedModel = record_type(:Input, Damping)\n@show closure(Damping)\n\nparameters = (\n    t_step = 0.001u\"s\",\n    t_end = 1.0u\"s\",\n    spring_constant = 250.0u\"s^-2\",\n    initial_position = 1.0u\"m\",\n)\n\nfunction plot_result()\n    output = Time.run(Model(; parameters...)) |> collect\n    damped = Time.run(DampedModel(; parameters..., damping = 3.0u\"s^-1\")) |> collect\n\n    fig = Figure()\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = \"time [s]\", ylabel = \"position [m]\")\n    for (label, run) in [\"spring\" => output, \"damped spring\" => damped]\n        lines!(ax, [s.time for s in run] ./ u\"s\", [s.position for s in run] ./ u\"m\";\n               label = label)\n    end\n    axislegend(ax)\n    save(\"docs/src/fig/composition-spring.svg\", fig)\nend\n\nplot_result()\n\nclosure(Damping) = (Main.Time, Main.Spring, Main.Damping)\n\n(Image: Spring, with and without damping)","title":"Extending from outside"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Compared-to-@compose","page":"Composition by closure","text":"What stays the same:\n\nComponents are modules. As with @compose, a component is a module, and the structs it defines are merged with those of the same name in other components.\nDiamonds and transitive closure. A model names only the components it needs directly, and shared dependencies appear once.\nA flat user interface. Models are still constructed with flat keyword arguments, and code still reads input.t_step.\n\nWhat changes:\n\nParts keep their types. A record is not merged into a single struct: each part keeps its own type, so behaviour is selected by dispatch instead of @for_each over module names.\nSubstitution through roles. A component can depend on a role, and any component implementing it fills the role for every dependant at once (see #4).\nNo code generation from stored syntax. There is no Core.eval of parent ASTs, and no copied using statements, so components can be defined anywhere (see #16).\nOrdinary Julia tooling applies. Struct definitions are written out where they are defined, so they can be found, documented and revised like any other struct.\n\nLimitations shared with @compose:\n\nField names must be unique across a record. Forwarding needs to know which part owns a name, just as @compose needs it to merge fields. record_type checks this.\nDependency order is the only order. step! runs components in dependency order. A model that needs a different order defines its own step!, as @compose models do now.\n\nBoth depend on which components end up in the model, so they can only be checked or resolved when the model is assembled.","title":"Compared to @compose"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Design-notes","page":"Composition by closure","text":"","title":"Design notes"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Roles-as-traits","page":"Composition by closure","text":"An earlier version of this prototype used subtyping as the only way to fill a role. Since a Julia struct has one supertype, chosen where the struct is defined, a component could fill only one role. Now that a component is a module, rather than one of its structs, subtyping doesn't apply at all, and role membership is the trait implements(::Val{M}, R) (see Requirements and roles). It can be extended anywhere, and a component can fill any number of roles. The example One component, two roles shows it in use. Two consequences of this design:\n\nimplements is user-extensible, so it can't be called from the generator of a @generated function. Roles are therefore resolved in record_type and stored in the composite type as a role map. A role that no component in the model requires is still found by component, but at run time.\nMethods can't dispatch on a role (value(s::AbstractSource, model) matches no part). Where that's needed, the usual Holy-trait pattern applies. In practice components find each other through component(model, Role).","title":"Roles as traits"},{"category":"section","location":"41-composition/#Components-as-modules","page":"Composition by closure","text":"An earlier version of this prototype identified a component by a type, its Input struct. That gave Input a special place: it was the component's identity, and the only record that could be composed at the top level. Other structs, like Facies, could only be composed as items inside an Input, through an extra protocol. Identifying a component by its module, as @compose does, removes both: all structs in a component are treated alike, and a record is any struct name that components share. The state of the spring example could be composed with record_type(:State, Spring) in the same way, though there map_components is more convenient, since each part of the state is computed from the input.\n\nThe cost is that a component is always a whole module, that roles can't be filled by subtyping, and that looking up a part by its module is only type-stable when the module is passed as Val(M) (see Composite values). The @component macro of the next chapter makes components written this way more concise.\n\n","title":"Components as modules"},{"category":"section","location":"90-contributing/#contributing","page":"Contributing guidelines","text":"First of all, thanks for the interest!\n\nWe welcome all kinds of contribution, including, but not limited to code, documentation, examples, configuration, issue creating, etc.\n\nBe polite and respectful, and follow the code of conduct.","title":"Contributing guidelines"},{"category":"section","location":"90-contributing/#Bug-reports-and-discussions","page":"Contributing guidelines","text":"If you think you found a bug, feel free to open an issue. Focused suggestions and requests can also be opened as issues. Before opening a pull request, start an issue or a discussion on the topic, please.","title":"Bug reports and discussions"},{"category":"section","location":"90-contributing/#Working-on-an-issue","page":"Contributing guidelines","text":"If you found an issue that interests you, comment on that issue what your plans are. If the solution to the issue is clear, you can immediately create a pull request (see below). Otherwise, say what your proposed solution is and wait for a discussion around it.\n\ntip: Tip\nFeel free to ping us after a few days if there are no responses.\n\nIf your solution involves code (or something that requires running the package locally), check the developer documentation. Otherwise, you can use the GitHub interface directly to create your pull request.\n\n","title":"Working on an issue"},{"category":"section","location":"16-tutorial-syntax/#Tutorial:-Syntax","page":"Tutorial: Syntax","text":"This tutorial repeats the previous one step by step, using the @component macro from ModuleMixins.Syntactic. The macro is shorthand only: every @component module expands to a plain module plus a few methods of requires and implements, exactly the ones we wrote by hand before. You can mix both styles.","title":"Tutorial: Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"16-tutorial-syntax/#Composing-Structs","page":"Tutorial: Syntax","text":"Modules A and B each define a struct S. Since they have no dependencies, @component doesn't add anything yet.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial-syntax1.jl\n#| id: tutorial-syntax1\nusing ModuleMixins.Syntactic\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\n\n@component module A\n    @kwdef struct S\n        x::Int\n    end\nend\n\n@component module B\n    @kwdef struct S\n        y::Int\n    end\nend\n\nModule C merges A and B. Instead of importing requires and extending it, we write @mixin A, B. This imports A and B from the parent module and declares them as requirements.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax1\n@component module C\n    @mixin A, B\nend\n\nThe record type is created just like before.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax1\nconst Model = record_type(:S, C)\n\nm = Model(x = 6, y = 7)\nprint(\"x = $(m.x), y = $(m.y)\")","title":"Composing Structs"},{"category":"section","location":"16-tutorial-syntax/#Inheritance","page":"Tutorial: Syntax","text":"Both A and B depend on a common component Time.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial-syntax2.jl\n#| id: tutorial-syntax2\nusing ModuleMixins.Syntactic\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\n\n@component module Time\n    @kwdef struct S\n        t::Int\n    end\nend\n\n@component module A\n    @mixin Time\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        x::Int\n    end\nend\n\n@component module B\n    @mixin Time\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        y::Int\n    end\nend\n\nThe @mixin declaration expands to the same requires(::Val{A}) = (Time,) that we wrote by hand.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax2\n@show requires(A)\n\nThe rest is unchanged.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax2\nconst Model = record_type(:S, A, B)\n\nm = Model(t = 1, x = 6, y = 7)\nprintln(\"t = $(m.t), x = $(m.x), y = $(m.y)\")\n\n@show parts(m)\n@show component(m, A)\n@show component(m, Time)","title":"Inheritance"},{"category":"section","location":"16-tutorial-syntax/#Abstraction","page":"Tutorial: Syntax","text":"The role AbstractBuffer and its interface are defined as before. Defining a role doesn't involve any macros.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial-syntax3.jl\n#| id: tutorial-syntax3\nusing ModuleMixins.Syntactic\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\n\nabstract type AbstractBuffer end\nfunction buffer_push! end\nfunction buffer_pop! end\nbuffer_push!(m, i) = buffer_push!(component(m, AbstractBuffer), i)\nbuffer_pop!(m) = buffer_pop!(component(m, AbstractBuffer))\n\nA component declares that it fills a role with @implements. We still need to import the role and the interface methods we extend: @component only takes care of requires and implements.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax3\n@component module ConstBuffer\n    import ..buffer_push!, ..buffer_pop!, ..AbstractBuffer\n    @implements AbstractBuffer\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        buffer_value::Int = 0\n    end\n\n    buffer_push!(s::S, item::Int) = nothing\n    buffer_pop!(s::S) = s.buffer_value\nend\n\n@component module VectorBuffer\n    import ..buffer_push!, ..buffer_pop!, ..AbstractBuffer\n    @implements AbstractBuffer\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        buffer::Vector{Int} = []\n    end\n\n    buffer_push!(s::S, item::Int) = push!(s.buffer, item)\n    buffer_pop!(s::S) = pop!(s.buffer)\nend\n\nA dependency on a role is declared with @requires. Where @mixin names a component and imports it, @requires takes any requirement, component or role, and imports nothing.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax3\n@component module A\n    import ..AbstractBuffer, ..buffer_push!, ..buffer_pop!\n    @requires AbstractBuffer\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        repeat::Int\n    end\n\n    function run(model)\n        for i in 1:model.repeat\n            buffer_push!(model, i)\n            r = buffer_pop!(model)\n            println(r)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nComposing the models is the same as before.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax3\nconst ConstModel = record_type(:S, A, ConstBuffer)\nA.run(ConstModel(repeat=5, buffer_value=9))\n\nconst VectorModel = record_type(:S, A, VectorBuffer)\nA.run(VectorModel(repeat=5))","title":"Abstraction"},{"category":"section","location":"16-tutorial-syntax/#Default-Implementation","page":"Tutorial: Syntax","text":"A default implementation of a role doesn't belong to any single component, so there's no syntax for it: we extend default_implementation just like in the procedural version.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax3\nimport ModuleMixins.Procedural: default_implementation\n\ndefault_implementation(::Type{AbstractBuffer}) = ConstBuffer\nconst DefaultModel = record_type(:S, A)\nA.run(DefaultModel(repeat=5))\n\nThe macro also catches some mistakes early. For instance, @implements with something that isn't a type, or @mixin with something that isn't a module, raises an error as soon as the component is defined, instead of when the model is composed.","title":"Default Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"16-tutorial-syntax/#Multiple-Types","page":"Tutorial: Syntax","text":"Now for the family of interacting types: an Input containing a list of agent specifications. Each component may contribute fields to the Input, to the AgentSpec, or to both.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial-syntax4.jl\n#| id: tutorial-syntax4\nusing ModuleMixins.Syntactic\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\n\n@component module AgentBase\n    @kwdef struct AgentSpec\n        name::String\n    end\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        agent_specs::Vector{AgentSpec} = []\n    end\nend\n\nIn the procedural version we had to make AgentSpec a type parameter of Input ourselves. The @component macro sees that a field of Input mentions AgentSpec, another struct in the same module, and adds the type parameter for us, so Input is really Input{AgentSpec}.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax4\n@show AgentBase.Input isa UnionAll\n@show AgentBase.Input.var\n\nOther components add their own fields to AgentSpec and Input by defining structs of the same name. A component doesn't need to contribute to every record: Movement only adds a field to AgentSpec.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax4\n@component module Movement\n    @mixin AgentBase\n\n    @kwdef struct AgentSpec\n        speed::Float64 = 1.0\n    end\nend\n\n@component module Lifetime\n    @mixin AgentBase\n\n    @kwdef struct AgentSpec\n        max_age::Int\n    end\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        n_steps::Int\n    end\nend\n\nWhen we create the Input record, AgentSpec is filled in with the composite of all AgentSpec parts. The agent specifications can be given as named tuples, which are converted to the composite type.\n\n#| id: tutorial-syntax4\nconst Model = record_type(:Input, Movement, Lifetime)\n\nm = Model(\n    n_steps = 10,\n    agent_specs = [\n        (name = \"rabbit\", speed = 2.0, max_age = 5),\n        (name = \"turtle\", speed = 0.1, max_age = 100)])\n\nfor a in m.agent_specs\n    println(\"$(a.name): speed $(a.speed), lives $(a.max_age) steps\")\nend","title":"Multiple Types"},{"category":"section","location":"16-tutorial-syntax/#Composing-Methods","page":"Tutorial: Syntax","text":"To call a method on every part of a composite, we use map_components. Here initial_state turns an Input into a mutable State. Every component turns its own Input part into its own State part, and the results are composed into a new Composite. The method definitions are plain Julia, so they look the same as in the procedural version.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial-syntax5.jl\nusing ModuleMixins.Syntactic\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\n\ninitial_state(part, input) = nothing\ninitial_state(input::Composite) = map_components(p -> initial_state(p, input), input)\n\n@component module A\n    import ..initial_state\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        x0::Int\n    end\n\n    @kwdef mutable struct State\n        x::Int\n    end\n\n    initial_state(::Input, input) = State(x = input.x0)\nend\n\n@component module B\n    import ..initial_state\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        y0::Int\n    end\n\n    @kwdef mutable struct State\n        y::Int\n    end\n\n    initial_state(::Input, input) = State(y = input.y0)\nend\n\n@component module Model\n    @mixin A, B\nend\n\nconst Input = record_type(:Input, Model)\nconst State = record_type(:State, Model)\n\nstate = initial_state(Input(x0 = 6, y0 = 7))\nprintln(state)\n@show state isa State\n\n","title":"Composing Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Composition-syntax","page":"Composition syntax","text":"note: Origin\nThis chapter, including the code in src/Components/Syntactic.jl, was written by Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic) at the request of Johan Hidding, on 2026-11-04.\n\nThe previous chapter composes models from components through a protocol of ordinary functions, requires and implements, and composes records from the structs that components define. This chapter adds the @component macro, an optional shorthand for that protocol.\n\n@component module Spring\n    @mixin Time\n    @implements AbstractOscillator\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        spring_constant::Float64\n    end\nend\n\nThe macro differs from @compose in one important way: it is purely local. It reads only the module it's applied to, and expands to that module plus a few method definitions. It never inspects or re-evaluates code from other modules. A component written by hand, like Damping in the previous chapter, works alongside components written with @component.","title":"Composition syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Syntax","page":"Composition syntax","text":"Inside @component module Name ... end:\n\nDeclaration Expands to\n@mixin A, B import ..A, ..B and requires(::Val{Name}) = (A, B)\n@requires R adds R (a role or a component module) to requires\n@implements R, S implements(::Val{Name}, ::Type{R}) = true, and the same for S\na field type mentions struct S of the same module the struct gets a type parameter S\n\nDeclarations can be separated by commas or spaces. @mixin A imports A from the parent module, just like the @mixin of @compose does. A qualified name, like @mixin Main.Spring or @mixin SomePackage.Spring, is not imported, and should resolve as written inside the component module. All method definitions refer to ModuleMixins.Procedural directly, so a component module needn't import requires or implements.\n\nThe macro also checks for mistakes when the component is defined:\n\na @mixin has to name a module;\na @requires has to name a module or a type;\nan @implements has to name a type.","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Contained-records","page":"Composition syntax","text":"A record contains another record when the type of one of its fields mentions the other record's struct. The previous chapter requires the containing struct to be parametric, with the parameter named after the contained record (see Records). The macro does this rewrite, so users don't see the type parameter. This works in any struct, not only Input. A struct that mentions its own name is a recursive type, not a record containing itself, and is left alone.\n\n# written\n@component module FaciesBase\n    @kwdef struct Facies\n        name::String\n    end\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        facies::Vector{Facies} = []\n    end\nend\n\n# expanded\nmodule FaciesBase\n    @kwdef struct Facies\n        name::String\n    end\n\n    @kwdef struct Input{Facies}\n        facies::Vector{Facies} = []\n    end\nend\n\nA component that adds per-facies fields only needs to define its own struct Facies. Components that don't contribute to the facies define no such struct.\n\nA struct may have type parameters of its own, like the Storage parameter that record_type sets by keyword. The record parameters are added after them.\n\n# written\n@kwdef struct State{Storage <: AbstractVector{Float64}}\n    facies::Vector{Facies} = []\n    height::Storage\nend\n\n# expanded\n@kwdef struct State{Storage <: AbstractVector{Float64}, Facies}\n    facies::Vector{Facies} = []\n    height::Storage\nend","title":"Contained records"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Implementation","page":"Composition syntax","text":"details: src/Components/Syntactic.jl\n#| file: src/Components/Syntactic.jl\nmodule Syntactic\n\nusing ..Common: strip_syntax_version\nimport ..Procedural\n\nexport @component\n\n<<syntactic-declarations>>\n<<syntactic-structs>>\n<<syntactic-checks>>\n<<syntactic-component>>\n\nend","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Declarations","page":"Composition syntax","text":"A declaration is a macro call at the top level of the module body. The macros @mixin, @requires and @implements are never expanded: @component sees the body before expansion, and removes them. They are defined only to give a helpful error when used elsewhere.\n\n#| id: syntactic-declarations\nconst DECLARATIONS = (Symbol(\"@mixin\"), Symbol(\"@requires\"), Symbol(\"@implements\"))\n\n\"\"\"\n    declaration(expr)\n\nIf `expr` is one of the declarations `@mixin`, `@requires` or `@implements`,\nreturn the declaration name and its arguments. Otherwise return `nothing`.\nArguments may be separated by spaces or by commas.\n\"\"\"\nfunction declaration(expr)\n    Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall) && expr.args[1] in DECLARATIONS || return nothing\n    args = [a for a in expr.args[2:end] if !(a isa LineNumberNode)]\n    args = collect(Iterators.flatten(Meta.isexpr(a, :tuple) ? a.args : (a,) for a in args))\n    return expr.args[1] => args\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    @mixin Components...\n\nDeclaration inside a `@component` module. See [`@component`](@ref).\n\"\"\"\nmacro mixin(args...)\n    error(\"`@mixin` can only be used inside a `@component` module\")\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    @requires Requirements...\n\nDeclaration inside a `@component` module. See [`@component`](@ref).\n\"\"\"\nmacro requires(args...)\n    error(\"`@requires` can only be used inside a `@component` module\")\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    @implements Roles...\n\nDeclaration inside a `@component` module. See [`@component`](@ref).\n\"\"\"\nmacro implements(args...)\n    error(\"`@implements` can only be used inside a `@component` module\")\nend","title":"Declarations"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Structs","page":"Composition syntax","text":"Structs may be wrapped in macro calls, like @kwdef or a docstring. map_struct looks through those, and applies a function to the struct definition. Only the top level of the module body is searched.\n\n#| id: syntactic-structs\n\"\"\"\n    map_struct(f, expr)\n\nApply `f` to the struct definition in `expr`, looking through macro calls\nlike `@kwdef` and docstrings. Returns `expr` unchanged if it doesn't define\na struct.\n\"\"\"\nfunction map_struct(f, expr)\n    Meta.isexpr(expr, :struct) && return f(expr)\n    Meta.isexpr(expr, :macrocall) || return expr\n    return Expr(:macrocall, (map_struct(f, a) for a in expr.args)...)\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    struct_name(expr)\n\nThe name of the struct defined by `expr`, or `nothing`.\n\"\"\"\nfunction struct_name(expr)\n    result = nothing\n    map_struct(s -> (result = type_name(s.args[2]); s), expr)\n    return result\nend\n\ntype_name(head) =\n    Meta.isexpr(head, :<:) ? type_name(head.args[1]) :\n    Meta.isexpr(head, :curly) ? head.args[1] :\n    head\n\nTo find the records a struct contains, collect the names of other structs in the module that appear in its field types. A field is name::T, possibly with a default value (@kwdef) or marked const. Other expressions in the struct body, like inner constructors, are skipped.\n\n#| id: syntactic-structs\n\"\"\"\n    field_types(struct_expr)\n\nThe declared types of the fields of a struct.\n\"\"\"\nfunction field_types(expr)\n    types = []\n    for field in expr.args[3].args\n        Meta.isexpr(field, :const) && (field = field.args[1])\n        Meta.isexpr(field, :(=)) && (field = field.args[1])\n        Meta.isexpr(field, :(::)) && length(field.args) == 2 && push!(types, field.args[2])\n    end\n    return types\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    mentioned(names, expr)\n\nThe elements of `names` that occur as a symbol anywhere in `expr`, in the\norder of `names`.\n\"\"\"\nfunction mentioned(names, expr)\n    found = Set{Symbol}()\n    visit(x) = x isa Symbol ? push!(found, x) : x isa Expr && foreach(visit, x.args)\n    visit(expr)\n    return [n for n in names if n in found]\nend\n\nAdding type parameters to the struct head keeps a supertype in place. If the struct already has type parameters, the record parameters are appended, except those that were already written by hand.\n\n#| id: syntactic-structs\nparameter_name(p) = Meta.isexpr(p, :<:) || Meta.isexpr(p, :>:) ? p.args[1] : p\n\nfunction add_parameters(head, params)\n    Meta.isexpr(head, :<:) && return Expr(:<:, add_parameters(head.args[1], params), head.args[2])\n    Meta.isexpr(head, :curly) || return Expr(:curly, head, params...)\n    own = parameter_name.(head.args[2:end])\n    return Expr(:curly, head.args..., filter(!in(own), params)...)\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    parameterize(expr, struct_names)\n\nRewrite the struct defined in `expr` (if any), so that every other struct in\n`struct_names` that is mentioned in a field type becomes a type parameter.\n\"\"\"\nparameterize(expr, struct_names) = map_struct(expr) do s\n    own = type_name(s.args[2])\n    contained = mentioned(filter(!=(own), struct_names), Expr(:block, field_types(s)...))\n    isempty(contained) && return s\n    return Expr(:struct, s.args[1], add_parameters(s.args[2], contained), s.args[3])\nend","title":"Structs"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Checks","page":"Composition syntax","text":"Checks that need the value of a name can't be done during macro expansion, since the name may not be defined yet. They are emitted as function calls at the end of the module, so they run when the module is defined.\n\n#| id: syntactic-checks\n\"\"\"\n    check_declaration(x, valid, declaration, name)\n\nCheck that `x`, named `name` in `declaration`, is an instance of `valid`.\n\"\"\"\nfunction check_declaration(x, valid, declaration, name)\n    x isa valid || error(\"$(declaration) $(name): expected a $(valid), got $(typeof(x))\")\n    return nothing\nend","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#The-macro","page":"Composition syntax","text":"@component splits the module body into declarations and other statements, rewrites the structs that contain records, and appends the method definitions. Inside the module, its own name refers to the module, so the methods dispatch on Val{Name}. Method names are GlobalRefs to Procedural, so they extend the protocol functions whatever is in scope in the component module.\n\n#| id: syntactic-component\nmixin_import(m::Symbol) = [Expr(:import, Expr(:., :., :., m))]\nmixin_import(_) = []\n\n\"\"\"\n    @component module Name\n        [@mixin Components...]\n        [@requires Requirements...]\n        [@implements Roles...]\n        ...\n    end\n\nDefine a component module. The declarations expand into methods of\n`ModuleMixins.Procedural`:\n\n- `@mixin A, B`: import `A` and `B` from the parent module (if they are plain\n  names), and require them as components.\n- `@requires R`: require role or component `R`.\n- `@implements R`: the component implements role `R`.\n\nIf a field type of a struct mentions another struct `S` of the same module,\nthe struct gets a type parameter `S`, which `record_type` fills with the\ncomposite of all parts of record `S`.\n\"\"\"\nmacro component(mod)\n    mod = strip_syntax_version(mod)\n    Meta.isexpr(mod, :module) || error(\"@component expects a module definition\")\n    bare, name, body = mod.args\n\n    mixins, requirements, roles, statements = [], [], [], []\n    for expr in body.args\n        decl = declaration(expr)\n        if decl === nothing\n            push!(statements, expr)\n        elseif decl.first === Symbol(\"@mixin\")\n            append!(mixins, decl.second)\n        elseif decl.first === Symbol(\"@requires\")\n            append!(requirements, decl.second)\n        else\n            append!(roles, decl.second)\n        end\n    end\n\n    struct_names = filter(!isnothing, struct_name.(statements))\n    statements = map(expr -> parameterize(expr, struct_names), statements)\n\n    P(f) = GlobalRef(Procedural, f)\n    check(x, valid, decl) =\n        :($(GlobalRef(@__MODULE__, :check_declaration))($x, $valid, $decl, $(QuoteNode(x))))\n    self = :(::$(GlobalRef(Base, :Val)){$name})\n    requirement_list = [mixins; requirements]\n\n    definitions = [\n        (check(m, Module, \"@mixin\") for m in mixins)...,\n        (check(r, Union{Module, Type}, \"@requires\") for r in requirements)...,\n        (check(r, Type, \"@implements\") for r in roles)...,\n        (isempty(requirement_list) ? () : (:($(P(:requires))($self) = ($(requirement_list...),)),))...,\n        (:($(P(:implements))($self, ::Type{$r}) = true) for r in roles)...,\n    ]\n\n    new_body = Expr(:block,\n        Iterators.flatten(mixin_import.(mixins))...,\n        statements...,\n        definitions...)\n    return esc(Expr(:toplevel, Expr(:module, bare, name, new_body)))\nend","title":"The macro"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Tests","page":"Composition syntax","text":"The tests repeat the elemental example of the previous chapter, mixing components written with @component and by hand. The last part tests records.\n\n#| file: test/SyntacticSpec.jl\nmodule SyntacticSpec\nusing Test\nusing ModuleMixins.Syntactic\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\nimport ModuleMixins.Procedural: default_implementation, requires\n\nabstract type AbstractSource end\nabstract type AbstractGradient end\nfunction value end\nfunction gradient end\n\n@component module Ramp\n    import ..AbstractSource, ..AbstractGradient, ..value, ..gradient\n    @implements AbstractSource, AbstractGradient\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        slope::Float64 = 1.0\n        x::Float64 = 0.0\n    end\n    value(s::Input, model) = s.slope * s.x\n    gradient(s::Input, model) = s.slope\nend\n\n@component module Constant\n    import ..AbstractSource, ..value\n    @implements AbstractSource\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        c::Float64 = 1.0\n    end\n    value(s::Input, model) = s.c\nend\n\ndefault_implementation(::Type{AbstractSource}) = Constant\n\n@component module Double\n    import ..AbstractSource, ..value\n    using ModuleMixins.Procedural: component\n    @requires AbstractSource\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        factor::Float64 = 2.0\n    end\n    value(d::Input, model) = d.factor * value(component(model, AbstractSource), model)\nend\n\n# written by hand\nmodule Square\n    import ..AbstractSource, ..value, ..requires\n    using ModuleMixins.Procedural: component\n    struct Input end\n    requires(::Val{Square}) = (AbstractSource,)\n    value(::Input, model) = value(component(model, AbstractSource), model)^2\nend\n\n@component module Sum\n    import ..value\n    using ModuleMixins.Procedural: component\n    @mixin Double Square\n    struct Input end\n    value(::Input, model) =\n        value(component(model, Double.Input), model) +\n        value(component(model, Square.Input), model)\nend\n\n@testset \"Syntactic\" begin\n    @test requires(Sum) == (Double, Square)\n    @test requires(Double) == (AbstractSource,)\n    @test implements(Ramp, AbstractGradient)\n    @test !implements(Constant, AbstractGradient)\n    @test closure(Sum) == (Constant, Double, Square, Sum)\n\n    m = record_type(:Input, Sum)(c = 3.0, factor = 10.0)\n    @test value(component(m, Sum.Input), m) == 39.0\n    r = record_type(:Input, Sum, Ramp)(slope = 2.0, x = 1.5)\n    @test (@inferred value(component(r, Sum.Input), r)) == 15.0\n    @test component(r, AbstractGradient) === component(r, AbstractSource)\nend\n\n@testset \"Syntactic errors\" begin\n    @test_throws ErrorException macroexpand(@__MODULE__, :(@component struct Input end))\n    @test_throws ErrorException @eval @component module BadMixin\n        @mixin AbstractSource\n        struct Input end\n    end\n    @test_throws ErrorException @eval @component module BadRequirement\n        @requires Base.sin\n        struct Input end\n    end\n    @test_throws ErrorException @eval @component module BadRole\n        @implements Base.sin\n        struct Input end\n    end\n    @test_throws ErrorException macroexpand(@__MODULE__, :(Syntactic.@mixin Sum))\nend\n\n@component module FaciesBase\n    @kwdef struct Layer\n        thickness::Float64 = 1.0\n    end\n\n    @kwdef struct Facies\n        name::String\n        layers::Vector{Layer} = []\n    end\n\n    \"\"\"\n    Input with a docstring and a supertype.\n    \"\"\"\n    @kwdef struct Input <: Any\n        facies::Vector{Facies} = []\n    end\n\n    # recursive, not a record containing itself\n    struct Tree\n        children::Vector{Tree}\n    end\nend\n\n@component module Production\n    @mixin FaciesBase\n    @kwdef struct Facies\n        rate::Float64 = 0.0\n    end\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        sea_level::Float64 = 0.0\n    end\n    @kwdef mutable struct State\n        height::Float64 = 0.0\n    end\nend\n\n# contributes nothing per facies\n@component module Time\n    @mixin FaciesBase\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        t_end::Float64 = 1.0\n    end\nend\n\n@component module Storing\n    @mixin FaciesBase\n    @kwdef struct Facies\n        depth::Float64 = 0.0\n    end\n    @kwdef struct State{Storage <: AbstractVector{Float64}}\n        facies::Vector{Facies} = []\n        level::Storage\n    end\n    # record parameter already written\n    @kwdef struct Input{Facies, Storage}\n        more_facies::Vector{Facies} = []\n        weights::Storage\n    end\nend\n\n@testset \"Syntactic records\" begin\n    @test FaciesBase.Input isa UnionAll\n    @test FaciesBase.Facies isa UnionAll\n    @test FaciesBase.Layer isa DataType\n    @test FaciesBase.Tree isa DataType\n\n    M = record_type(:Input, Production, Time)\n    m = M(facies = [(name = \"reef\", rate = 2.0, layers = [(thickness = 3.0,)]), (name = \"lagoon\",)],\n          t_end = 5.0)\n    @test m.t_end == 5.0\n    @test [f.name for f in m.facies] == [\"reef\", \"lagoon\"]\n    @test [f.rate for f in m.facies] == [2.0, 0.0]\n    @test m.facies[1].layers[1].thickness == 3.0\n    @test isempty(m.facies[2].layers)\n    @test component(m.facies[1], Production) isa Production.Facies\n    @test component(m, FaciesBase) isa FaciesBase.Input\n    @test (@inferred (m -> m.facies[1].rate)(m)) == 2.0\n\n    S = record_type(:State, Production, Time)\n    @test S(height = 1.0).height == 1.0\n\n    @test Storing.State isa UnionAll\n    @test Storing.State.var.name === :Storage\n    @test Storing.State.body.var.name === :Facies\n    @test Storing.Input.var.name === :Facies\n    @test Storing.Input.body.var.name === :Storage\n    P = record_type(:State, Storing; Storage = Vector{Float64})\n    @test isconcretetype(P)\n    p = P(facies = [(name = \"reef\", depth = 2.0)], level = [1.0])\n    @test p.level == [1.0] && p.facies[1].name == \"reef\" && p.facies[1].depth == 2.0\n\n    # only the base part\n    b = record_type(:Input, FaciesBase)(facies = [(name = \"a\",)])\n    @test b.facies[1].name == \"a\"\n    @test_throws ErrorException record_type(:Input, FaciesBase)(facies = [(rate = 1.0,)])\nend\nend","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Example:-facies","page":"Composition syntax","text":"A small model in the style of CarboKitten. Facies have a name, production adds a rate per facies, and the model computes the total production over all facies. Each component dispatches on its own Facies part, while reading other fields through the composite.\n\n#| file: examples/composition/facies.jl\nusing ModuleMixins.Syntactic\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\n\nfunction describe end\n\n@component module FaciesBase\n    import ..describe\n    @kwdef struct Facies\n        name::String\n    end\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        facies::Vector{Facies} = []\n    end\n    describe(f::Facies, item) = \"facies $(f.name)\"\nend\n\n@component module Production\n    import ..describe\n    @mixin FaciesBase\n    @kwdef struct Facies\n        rate::Float64 = 0.0\n    end\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        scale::Float64 = 1.0\n    end\n    describe(f::Facies, item) = \"produces $(f.rate)\"\n    total(input) = input.scale * sum(f.rate for f in input.facies)\nend\n\nconst Model = record_type(:Input, Production)\nm = Model(scale = 2.0, facies = [(name = \"reef\", rate = 3.0), (name = \"lagoon\", rate = 1.0)])\n@show Production.total(m)\nfor f in m.facies\n    println(join((describe(p, f) for p in parts(f)), \", \"))\nend\n\nProduction.total(m) = 8.0\nfacies reef, produces 3.0\nfacies lagoon, produces 1.0","title":"Example: facies"},{"category":"section","location":"42-composition-syntax/#Example:-GPU-storage","page":"Composition syntax","text":"The arrays of a model state can live on a GPU. Each part of the state takes the array type as a type parameter Storage, which record_type sets for the whole model. The state is built on the host, and moved to the device with adapt. The kernel, written with KernelAbstractions, takes the composites as they are: when they are passed to the kernel, Adapt converts their arrays to device arrays once more, and property access works on the device as well.\n\nWe use Float32, since many (integrated) GPUs don't support Float64. The example needs an environment with Adapt and KernelAbstractions, and CUDA or oneAPI to run on a GPU:\n\njulia --project=<env> examples/composition/gpu.jl [cpu|cuda|oneapi]\n\n#| file: examples/composition/gpu.jl\nusing ModuleMixins.Syntactic\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\nusing Adapt\nusing KernelAbstractions\n\nconst backend_name = get(ARGS, 1, \"cpu\")\nif backend_name == \"cuda\"\n    using CUDA\nelseif backend_name == \"oneapi\"\n    using oneAPI\nend\n\nconst DeviceArray =\n    backend_name == \"cuda\" ? CUDA.CuArray :\n    backend_name == \"oneapi\" ? oneAPI.oneArray :\n    Array\n\n@component module Grid\n    using Adapt\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        n::Int\n        dt::Float32 = 0.1f0\n    end\n\n    @kwdef struct State{Storage <: AbstractVector{Float32}}\n        height::Storage\n    end\n\n    Adapt.@adapt_structure State\nend\n\n@component module Growth\n    using Adapt\n    @mixin Grid\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        rate::Float32\n    end\n\n    @kwdef struct State{Storage <: AbstractVector{Float32}}\n        factor::Storage\n    end\n\n    Adapt.@adapt_structure State\nend\n\n@kernel function step_kernel!(state, input)\n    i = @index(Global)\n    @inbounds state.height[i] += input.dt * input.rate * state.factor[i]\nend\n\nconst Input = record_type(:Input, Growth)\nconst HostState = record_type(:State, Growth; Storage = Vector{Float32})\n\ninput = Input(n = 1024, rate = 2.0f0)\nhost = HostState(\n    height = zeros(Float32, input.n),\n    factor = collect(range(0.0f0, 1.0f0, length = input.n)))\n\nstate = adapt(DeviceArray, host)\nprintln(\"storage: \", typeof(component(state, Grid).height))\n\nbackend = get_backend(state.height)\nstep! = step_kernel!(backend)\nfor _ in 1:10\n    step!(state, input; ndrange = input.n)\nend\nKernelAbstractions.synchronize(backend)\n\nresult = adapt(Array, state)\nprintln(\"result isa HostState: \", result isa HostState)\nprintln(\"final height: \", result.height[end])\n\nAfter ten steps of 0.1, at a growth rate of 2 and a factor of 1, the last cell has a height of 2, up to rounding. With CUDA:\n\nstorage: CuArray{Float32, 1, CUDACore.DeviceMemory}\nresult isa HostState: true\nfinal height: 2.0000002\n\n","title":"Example: GPU storage"},{"category":"section","location":"15-tutorial/#Tutorial","page":"Tutorial","text":"note: New System\nThe new ModuleMixins system in version 0.4 has two components: a procedural part completely based on composition and dispatch, and a syntactic part that relieves some of the boilerplate.","title":"Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"15-tutorial/#Composing-Structs","page":"Tutorial","text":"We start small. We have two modules A and B, each with a struct S. The struct needs to be defined @kwdef.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial1.jl\n#| id: tutorial1\nmodule A\n    @kwdef struct S\n        x::Int\n    end\nend\n\nmodule B\n    @kwdef struct S\n        y::Int\n    end\nend\n\nNow we implement a module C that merges A and B. We indicate this relation by dispatching on the requires method.\n\n#| id: tutorial1\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\n\nmodule C\n    import ..requires, ..A, ..B\n    requires(::Val{C}) = (A, B)\nend\n\nWe can now instantiate a composite type using the record_type function.\n\n#| id: tutorial1\nconst Model = record_type(:S, C)\n\nm = Model(x = 6, y = 7)\nprint(\"x = $(m.x), y = $(m.y)\")\n\nnote: Unique field names\nIt is the responsibility of the user to have only unique field names. Having two parts contributing the same name to a record type is an error.","title":"Composing Structs"},{"category":"section","location":"15-tutorial/#Inheritance","page":"Tutorial","text":"Now, suppose both A and B depend on a common component Time.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial2.jl\n#| id: tutorial2\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\n\nmodule Time\n    @kwdef struct S\n        t::Int\n    end\nend\n\nmodule A\n    import ..requires, ..Time\n    requires(::Val{A}) = (Time,)\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        x::Int\n    end\nend\n\nmodule B\n    import ..requires, ..Time\n    requires(::Val{B}) = (Time,)\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        y::Int\n    end\nend     \n\nNow we can create our model again, this time directly from A and B.\n\n#| id: tutorial2\nconst Model = record_type(:S, A, B)\n\nm = Model(t = 1, x = 6, y = 7)\nprintln(\"t = $(m.t), x = $(m.x), y = $(m.y)\")\n\nThe Time component is included once as a transitive requirement.\n\n#| id: tutorial2\n@show parts(m)\n\nAnd we can lift parts of the struct.\n\n#| id: tutorial2\n@show component(m, A)\n@show component(m, Time)","title":"Inheritance"},{"category":"section","location":"15-tutorial/#Abstraction","page":"Tutorial","text":"Instead of directly depending on a module, it is possible to depend on a Role.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial3.jl\n#| id: tutorial3\nusing ModuleMixins.Procedural\n\nabstract type AbstractBuffer end\nbuffer_push!(m, i) = buffer_push!(component(m, AbstractBuffer), i)\nbuffer_pop!(m) = buffer_pop!(component(m, AbstractBuffer))\n\nThe implements method is used to indicate if a components implements a certain Role. Here we define two modules that implement the AbstractBuffer role. This role has two interfacing methods, for which we forward the call to their specialization.\n\nThe ConstBuffer ignores everything that comes in and just returns a preset buffer_value.\n\n#| id: tutorial3\nmodule ConstBuffer\n    import ..buffer_push!, ..buffer_pop!, ..implements, ..AbstractBuffer\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        buffer_value::Int = 0\n    end\n\n    buffer_push!(s::S, item::Int) = nothing\n    buffer_pop!(s::S) = s.buffer_value\n\n    implements(::Val{ConstBuffer}, ::Type{AbstractBuffer}) = true\nend\n\nThe VectorBuffer pushes and pops from a vector like you would expect.\n\n#| id: tutorial3\nmodule VectorBuffer\n    import ..buffer_push!, ..buffer_pop!, ..implements, ..AbstractBuffer\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        buffer::Vector{Int} = []\n    end\n\n    buffer_push!(s::S, item::Int) = push!(s.buffer, item)\n    buffer_pop!(s::S) = pop!(s.buffer)\n\n    implements(::Val{VectorBuffer}, ::Type{AbstractBuffer}) = true\nend\n\nThe main module A depends on AbstractBuffer and uses the defined methods to run a little loop.\n\n#| id: tutorial3\nmodule A\n    import ..requires, ..AbstractBuffer, ..buffer_push!, ..buffer_pop!\n\n    @kwdef struct S\n        repeat::Int\n    end\n\n    requires(::Val{A}) = (AbstractBuffer,)\n\n    function run(model)\n        for i in 1:model.repeat\n            buffer_push!(model, i)\n            r = buffer_pop!(model)\n            println(r)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\nNow we can compose two versions of our final \"Model\".\n\n#| id: tutorial3\nconst ConstModel = record_type(:S, A, ConstBuffer)\nA.run(ConstModel(repeat=5, buffer_value=9))\n\nconst VectorModel = record_type(:S, A, VectorBuffer)\nA.run(VectorModel(repeat=5))\n\nIt is illegal to instantiate a Composite type where not all required roles are fulfilled. Running record_type(:S, A) will trigger the error: ERROR: no component implementing role AbstractBuffer was selected, and there is no default.","title":"Abstraction"},{"category":"section","location":"15-tutorial/#Default-Implementation","page":"Tutorial","text":"You may specify a default implementation of a role by dispatching the default_implementation method.\n\n#| id: tutorial3\nimport ModuleMixins.Procedural: default_implementation\n\ndefault_implementation(::Type{AbstractBuffer}) = ConstBuffer\nconst DefaultModel = record_type(:S, A)\nA.run(DefaultModel(repeat=5))\n\nnote: One part per role\nWhen two selected components both implement the same role, an error is raised.","title":"Default Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"15-tutorial/#Multiple-Types","page":"Tutorial","text":"In some cases components affect not a single type, but a family of interacting types. For instance, in CarboKitten (the motivating case for ModuleMixins) we have a large Input struct that contains a list of Facies definitions. Similarly, when doing agent-based modelling, you'd have a list of agent specifications in your input.\n\nWe need the AgentSpec type to be a type variable of the Input type. The parameter name AgentSpec must be the same as the struct, as it is used to insert the correct composite type when record_type is called.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial4.jl\n#| id: tutorial4\nmodule AgentBase\n    @kwdef struct AgentSpec\n        name::String\n    end\n\n    @kwdef struct Input{AgentSpec}\n        agent_specs::Vector{AgentSpec}\n    end\nend\n\nWe can now expand on both the Input and AgentSpec structs.\n\n#| id: tutorial4\nmodule Movement\n    import ..requires, ..AgentBase\n\n    @kwdef struct AgentSpec\n        speed::Float64 = 1.0\n    end\n\n    requires(::Val{Movement}) = (AgentBase,)\nend\n\nmodule Lifetime\n    import ..requires, ..AgentBase\n\n    @kwdef struct AgentSpec\n        max_age::Int\n    end\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        n_steps::Int\n    end\n\n    requires(::Val{Lifetime}) = (AgentBase,)\nend\n\nThe rest still works as expected.\n\n#| id: tutorial4\nconst Model = record_type(:Input, Movement, Lifetime)\n\nm = Model(\n    n_steps = 10,\n    agent_specs = [\n        (name = \"rabbit\", speed = 2.0, max_age = 5),\n        (name = \"turtle\", speed = 0.1, max_age = 100)])\n\nfor a in m.agent_specs\n    println(\"$(a.name): speed $(a.speed), lives $(a.max_age) steps\")\nend\n\nnote: Type arguments\nA type parameter that isn't named after a record is set by a keyword argument to record_type, for instance record_type(:State, Model; Storage = Vector{Float64}). Components that use the same parameter name share the setting. This is useful for storage that can live on the CPU or a GPU; see Example: GPU storage.","title":"Multiple Types"},{"category":"section","location":"15-tutorial/#Composing-Methods","page":"Tutorial","text":"Suppose we have a method that is defined on multiple component parts, and we want to call this method on every part automatically. One example we often see returning is to create a constructor for the composite type. In this case we turn an Input struct into a mutable State struct, using the initial_state method.\n\n#| file: examples/tutorial5.jl\nimport ModuleMixins.Procedural: map_components, record_type, Composite\n\ninitial_state(part, input) = nothing\ninitial_state(input::Composite) = map_components(p -> initial_state(p, input), input)\n\nmodule A\n    import ..initial_state\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        x0::Int\n    end\n\n    @kwdef mutable struct State\n        x::Int\n    end\n\n    initial_state(::Input, input) = State(x = input.x0)\nend\n\nmodule B\n    import ..initial_state\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        y0::Int\n    end\n\n    @kwdef mutable struct State\n        y::Int\n    end\n\n    initial_state(::Input, input) = State(y = input.y0)\nend\n\nmodule Model\n    import ..record_type, ..A, ..B\n    const Input = record_type(:Input, A, B)\n    const State = record_type(:State, A, B)\nend\n\nprintln(initial_state(Model.Input(x0=6, y0=7)))\n\n","title":"Composing Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"#ModuleMixins","page":"ModuleMixins","text":"Documentation for ModuleMixins.\n\nModuleMixins is a way of composing families of struct definitions on a module level. Suppose we have several modules that contain structs of the same name. We can compose these modules such that all the structs they have in common are merged. Methods that work on one component should now work on the composed type.\n\ninfo: Info\nMost problems with object type abstraction can be solved in Julia by cleverly using abstract types and multiple dispatch. Use ModuleMixins only after you have convinced yourself you absolutely need it.\n\nIntroduction\nExample\nImplementation","title":"ModuleMixins"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contributors","page":"ModuleMixins","text":"<!-- ALL-CONTRIBUTORS-LIST:START - Do not remove or modify this section -->\n<!-- prettier-ignore-start -->\n<!-- markdownlint-disable -->\n\n<!-- markdownlint-restore -->\n<!-- prettier-ignore-end -->\n\n<!-- ALL-CONTRIBUTORS-LIST:END -->\n\n","title":"Contributors"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Implementation","page":"Implementation","text":"","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Prelude","page":"Implementation","text":"#| file: src/ModuleMixins.jl\nmodule ModuleMixins\n\nmodule Common\n    \"\"\"\n    Generic function for converting syntax data objects to expressions.\n    \"\"\"\n    function to_expression end\n\n    \"\"\"\n        eval_to_expression(mod::Module, obj)\n\n    Generic function for converting syntax data objects to expressions,\n    where parts of expressions need to be evaluated in a given module.\n\n    Defaults to calling `to_expression`, dropping the `mod` argument.\n    \"\"\"\n    eval_to_expression(mod::Module, obj) = to_expression(obj)\n\n    \"\"\"\n        eval_to_expression(mod::Module)\n\n    Currying version of `eval_to_expression`.\n    \"\"\"\n    eval_to_expression(mod::Module) = obj -> eval_to_expression(mod, obj)\n\n    \"\"\"\n    Get name attribute from syntax data object.\n    \"\"\"\n    name(obj) = obj.name\n\n    \"\"\"\n        strip_syntax_version(expr)\n\n    From Julia 1.14, a `module` expression may carry the syntax version\n    of the code it was parsed from as its first argument:\n    `Expr(:module, v\"1.14.0\", true, name, body)`. Whether it does depends\n    on the project the code lives in, not on the Julia version alone.\n    Remove the version, so that `@capture` patterns of the form\n    `module name_ body__ end` match either way.\n    \"\"\"\n    strip_syntax_version(expr) =\n        Meta.isexpr(expr, :module) && !isempty(expr.args) && expr.args[1] isa VersionNumber ?\n            Expr(:module, expr.args[2:end]...) : expr\nend\n\ninclude(\"Passes.jl\")\ninclude(\"Spec.jl\")\ninclude(\"Templates.jl\")\ninclude(\"Mixins.jl\")\ninclude(\"Structs.jl\")\ninclude(\"Constructors.jl\")\ninclude(\"Components/Procedural.jl\")\ninclude(\"Components/Syntactic.jl\")\n\nusing MacroTools: @capture, postwalk\nimport .Common: name, strip_syntax_version\nimport .Passes: Pass, pass, no_match, walk\nimport .Mixins: MixinPass\nimport .Structs: Struct, CollectStructPass, define_struct\nimport .Constructors: Constructor, CollectConstructorPass, define_constructor\nimport .Templates: make_parameter, ModuleTemplate\n\nexport @compose, @for_each\n\n<<compose>>\n<<for-each>>\n\nend\n\nTo facilitate testing, we need to be able to compare syntax. We use the clean function to remove source information from expressions.\n\n#| file: test/runtests.jl\nusing Test\n\n@testset \"ModuleMixins.jl\" begin\n    include(\"SpecSpec.jl\")\n    include(\"TemplatesSpec.jl\")\n    include(\"PassesSpec.jl\")\n    include(\"MixinsSpec.jl\")\n    include(\"StructsSpec.jl\")\n    include(\"ConstructorsSpec.jl\")\n    include(\"ComposeSpec.jl\")\n    include(\"TemplateMixinSpec.jl\")\n    include(\"ProceduralSpec.jl\")\n    include(\"SyntacticSpec.jl\")\nend","title":"Prelude"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Mixin-Pass","page":"Implementation","text":"The MixinPass now filters for appearances of the @mixin <Component> syntax and transforms them into using ..<Component>. This assumes that the symbols used are visible in the parent module. In the case of a template mixin, the template instantiation is created with a unique name (using gensym) and imported aliased to the original name.\n\n#| file: src/Mixins.jl\nmodule Mixins\n\nusing MacroTools: @capture\nimport ..Common: to_expression, eval_to_expression, name, strip_syntax_version\nimport ..Passes: Pass, pass, no_match, walk\nusing ..Templates: Argument, eval_argument, bind\n\nabstract type AbstractMixin end\n\nstruct MixinModule <: AbstractMixin\n    name::Symbol\nend\n\nto_expression(t::MixinModule) = :(using ..$(t.name))\n\n<<template-expansion>>\n\n@kwdef struct MixinPass <: Pass\n    environment::Module\n    items::Vector{AbstractMixin}\nend\n\nfunction pass(m::MixinPass, expr)\n    @capture(expr, @mixin raw_deps_) || return no_match\n\n    deps = if @capture(raw_deps, (multiple_deps__,))\n        multiple_deps\n    else\n        [raw_deps]\n    end\n\n    targets = deps .|> parse_mixin_arg\n    append!(m.items, targets)\n    imports = targets .|> eval_to_expression(m.environment)\n    return :(begin\n        $(imports...)\n    end)\nend\n\nmacro spec_using(mod)\n    @assert @capture(strip_syntax_version(mod), module mod_name_ body__ end)\n\n    parents = MixinPass(__module__, [])\n    clean_body = walk(parents, body)\n\n    esc(Expr(:toplevel, :(module $mod_name\n        $(clean_body...)\n        const AST = $body\n        const PARENTS = [$(QuoteNode.(name.(parents.items))...)]\n    end)))\nend\n\nend","title":"Mixin Pass"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Test:-@spec_using","page":"Implementation","text":"I can't think of any usecase where a @mixin A, doesn't also mean using ..A. By replacing the @mixin with a using statement, we also no longer need to import @mixin. In fact, that macro becomes redundant. Also, in @spec_using we're allowed multiple @mixin statements.\n\n#| file: test/MixinsSpec.jl\nusing ModuleMixins.Mixins: @spec_using\n\n@testset \"ModuleMixins.Mixins\" begin\n    @spec_using module SU_A\n    const X = :hello\n    export X\n    end\n\n    @spec_using module SU_B\n    @mixin SU_A\n    const Y = X\n    end\n\n    @spec_using module SU_C\n    const Z = :goodbye\n    end\n\n    @spec_using module SU_D\n    @mixin SU_A\n    @mixin SU_B, SU_C\n    end\n\n    @testset \"@spec_using\" begin\n        @test SU_B.Y == SU_A.X\n        @test SU_B.PARENTS == [:SU_A]\n        @test SU_D.PARENTS == [:SU_A, :SU_B, :SU_C]\n        @test SU_D.SU_C.Z == :goodbye\n    end\nend","title":"Test: @spec_using"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Structure-of-structs","page":"Implementation","text":"We'll convert struct syntax into collectable data, then convert that back into structs again. We'll support several patterns:\n\ndetails: test/StructsSpec.jl\n#| file: test/StructsSpec.jl\n@testset \"ModuleMixins.Structs\" begin\n    using ModuleMixins.Structs: Struct, parse_struct, define_struct, mangle_type_parameters!\n    using MacroTools: prewalk, rmlines\n    clean(expr) = prewalk(rmlines, expr)\n\n    <<test-structs>>\nend\n\n#| id: test-structs\ncases = Dict(\n    :(struct A x end) => Struct(false, false, :A, nothing, nothing, [:x]),\n    :(mutable struct A x end) => Struct(false, true, :A, nothing, nothing, [:x]),\n    :(@kwdef struct A x end) => Struct(true, false, :A, nothing, nothing, [:x]),\n    :(@kwdef mutable struct A x end) => Struct(true, true, :A, nothing, nothing, [:x]),\n    :(struct A{T} x::T end) => Struct(false, false, :A, [:T], nothing, [:(x::T)]),\n)\n\nfor (k, v) in pairs(cases)\n    @testset \"Struct mangling: $(join(split(string(clean(k))), \" \"))\" begin\n        @test clean(define_struct(parse_struct(k))) == clean(k)\n        @test clean(define_struct(v)) == clean(k)\n    end\nend\n\nEach of these can have either just a Symbol for a name, or a A <: B expression. This is a bit cumbersome, but we'll have to deal with all of these cases.\n\n#| id: test-structs\n@testset \"Struct mangling abstracts\" begin\n    @test parse_struct(:(struct A <: B x end)).abstract_type == :B\n    @test parse_struct(:(mutable struct A <: B x end)).abstract_type == :B\nend\n\n@testset \"Mangling type arguments\" begin\n    let s = parse_struct(:(struct S{T} x::T end))\n        @test s.type_parameters == [:T]\n        mangle_type_parameters!(s, :A)\n        @test s.type_parameters == [:_T_A]\n        @test clean(define_struct(s)) == clean(:(struct S{_T_A} x::_T_A end))\n    end\n    let s = parse_struct(:(struct S{T} x::Vector{T} = [] end))\n        mangle_type_parameters!(s, :A)\n        @test clean(define_struct(s)) == clean(:(struct S{_T_A} x::Vector{_T_A} = [] end))\n    end\nend\n\n@testset \"Getting fieldnames\" begin\n    let s = parse_struct(:(struct S x; y; z end))\n        @test fieldnames(s) == [:x, :y, :z]\n    end\nend","title":"Structure of structs"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Implementation-2","page":"Implementation","text":"details: src/Structs.jl\n#| file: src/Structs.jl\nmodule Structs\n\nusing MacroTools: @capture, postwalk\nimport ..Passes: Pass, pass, no_match\n\n<<struct-data>>\n<<collect-struct-pass>>\n\nend\n\nWe need to store all information on a struct definition, so that we can reconstruct the original expression, or a similar expression with extended fields.\n\n#| id: struct-data\nmutable struct Struct\n    use_kwdef::Bool\n    is_mutable::Bool\n    name::Symbol\n    type_parameters::Union{Vector{Symbol},Nothing}\n    abstract_type::Union{Symbol,Nothing}\n    fields::Vector{Union{Expr,Symbol}}\nend\n\nIf we have a type parameter called T, we want to rename it so that it can't clash with previously defined type parameters.\n\n#| id: struct-data\nfunction mangle_type_parameters!(s::Struct, suffix::Symbol)\n    s.type_parameters === nothing && return\n\n    d = IdDict{Symbol, Symbol}(\n        (k => Symbol(\"_$(k)_$(suffix)\") for k in s.type_parameters)...)\n\n    replace_type_par(expr) =\n        postwalk(x -> x isa Symbol ? get(d, x, x) : x, expr)\n\n    s.fields = replace_type_par.(s.fields)\n    s.type_parameters = collect(values(d))\n    return s\nend\n\nfunction mappend(a::Union{Vector{T}, Nothing}, b::Union{Vector{T}, Nothing}) where T\n    isnothing(a) && return b\n    isnothing(b) && return a\n    return vcat(a, b)\nend\n\nfunction extend_struct!(s1::Struct, s2::Struct)\n    append!(s1.fields, s2.fields)\n    s1.type_parameters = mappend(s1.type_parameters, s2.type_parameters)\n    return s1\nend\n\nfunction parse_struct(expr)\n    @capture(expr, (@kwdef kw_struct_expr_) | struct_expr_)\n    uses_kwdef = kw_struct_expr !== nothing\n    struct_expr = uses_kwdef ? kw_struct_expr : struct_expr\n\n    @capture(struct_expr,\n        (struct name_ fields__ end) |\n        (mutable struct mut_name_ fields__ end)) || return\n\n    is_mutable = mut_name !== nothing\n    sname = is_mutable ? mut_name : name\n    @capture(sname, (pname_ <: abst_) | pname_)\n    @capture(pname, (name_{pars__}) | name_)\n\n    return Struct(uses_kwdef, is_mutable, name, pars, abst, fields)\nend\n\nfunction define_struct(s::Struct)\n    name = s.type_parameters !== nothing ? :($(s.name){$(s.type_parameters...)}) : s.name\n    name = s.abstract_type !== nothing ? :($(name) <: $(s.abstract_type)) : name\n    sdef = if s.is_mutable\n        :(mutable struct $name\n            $(s.fields...)\n        end)\n    else\n        :(struct $name\n            $(s.fields...)\n        end)\n    end\n    s.use_kwdef ? :(@kwdef $sdef) : sdef\nend\n\nfunction Base.fieldnames(s::Struct)\n    get_fieldname(def::Symbol) = def\n    function get_fieldname(def::Expr)\n        if @capture(def, name_::type_)\n            return name\n        end\n        if @capture(def, name_::type_ = default_)\n            return name\n        end\n        error(\"unknown struct field expression: $(def)\")\n    end\n\n    return get_fieldname.(s.fields)\nend","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Collecting-structs","page":"Implementation","text":"#| id: collect-struct-pass\nstruct CollectStructPass <: Pass\n    items::IdDict{Symbol,Struct}\n    name::Symbol\nend\n\nfunction pass(p::CollectStructPass, expr)\n    s = parse_struct(expr)\n    if s === nothing\n        return no_match\n    end\n\n    mangle_type_parameters!(s, p.name)\n    if s.name in keys(p.items)\n        extend_struct!(p.items[s.name], s)\n    else\n        p.items[s.name] = s\n    end\n    return nothing\nend","title":"Collecting structs"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#@compose","page":"Implementation","text":"Unfortunately now comes a big leap. We'll merge all struct definitions inside the body of a module definition with that of its parents. We must also make sure that a struct definition still compiles, so we have to take along using and const statements.\n\ndetails: test/ComposeSpec.jl\n#| file: test/ComposeSpec.jl\nusing ModuleMixins\n\n<<test-compose-toplevel>>\n\n@testset \"ModuleMixins.Compose\" begin\n    using ModuleMixins: @compose\n\n    <<test-compose>>\nend\n\n#| id: test-compose-toplevel\nmodule ComposeTest1\nusing ModuleMixins\n\n@compose module A\n    struct S\n        a::Int\n    end\nend\n\n@compose module B\n    struct S{T}\n        b::T\n    end\nend\n\n@compose module AB\n    @mixin A, B\nend\nend\n\n#| id: test-compose\n@testset \"compose struct members\" begin\n    @test ComposeTest1.AB.PARENTS == [:A, :B]\n    @test fieldnames(ComposeTest1.AB.S) == (:a, :b)\nend\n@testset \"compose hierarchy\" begin\n    @test ComposeTest1.AB.MIXIN_TREE == IdDict(:AB => [:A, :B], :A => [], :B => [])\n    @test WriterABC.MIXIN_TREE == IdDict(\n        :WriterABC => [:WriterB, :WriterC],\n        :WriterC => [],\n        :WriterB => [:WriterA],\n        :WriterA => [])\nend\n@testset \"composed struct has type parameter\" begin\n    @test ComposeTest1.AB.S{Float64}(1, 2).b isa Float64\nend\n\n#| id: compose\nstruct CollectUsingPass <: Pass\n    items::Vector{Expr}\nend\n\nfunction pass(p::CollectUsingPass, expr)\n    @capture(expr, using x__ | using mod__: x__) || return no_match\n    push!(p.items, expr)\n    return nothing\nend\n\nstruct CollectConstPass <: Pass\n    items::Vector{Expr}\nend\n\nfunction pass(p::CollectConstPass, expr)\n    @capture(expr, const x_ = y_) || return no_match\n    push!(p.items, expr)\n    return nothing\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    @compose module Name\n        [@mixin Parents, ...]\n        ...\n    end\n\nCreates a new composable module `Name`. Structs inside this module are\nmerged with those of the same name in `Parents`.\n\"\"\"\nmacro compose(mod)\n    @assert @capture(strip_syntax_version(mod), module mod_name_ body__ end)\n\n    <<catch-template-definition>>\n\n    mixins = Symbol[]\n    mixin_tree = IdDict{Symbol, Vector{Symbol}}()\n    parents = MixinPass(__module__, [])\n    usings = CollectUsingPass([])\n    consts = CollectConstPass([])\n    struct_items = IdDict{Symbol, Struct}()\n    constructor_items = IdDict{Symbol, Constructor}()\n\n    function mixin(expr; mod_name=mod_name)\n        structs = CollectStructPass(struct_items, mod_name)\n        constructors = CollectConstructorPass(constructor_items)\n        parents = MixinPass(__module__, [])\n        pass1 = walk(parents, expr)\n        mixin_tree[mod_name] = name.(parents.items)\n        for p in parents.items\n            name(p) in mixins && continue\n            push!(mixins, name(p))\n            parent_expr = Core.eval(__module__, :($(name(p)).AST))\n            mixin(parent_expr; mod_name=name(p))\n        end\n        walk(usings + consts + structs + constructors, pass1)\n    end\n\n    fields = CollectStructPass(IdDict{Symbol,Struct}(), mod_name)\n    walk(fields, body)\n\n    clean_body = mixin(body)\n\n    esc(Expr(:toplevel, :(module $mod_name\n        const AST = $body\n        const PARENTS = [$(QuoteNode.(mixins)...)]\n        const MIXIN_TREE = $(mixin_tree)\n        const FIELDS = $(IdDict((n => v.fields for (n, v) in pairs(fields.items))...))\n        const CONSTRUCTORS = $(constructor_items)\n        $(usings.items...)\n        $(consts.items...)\n        $(define_struct.(values(struct_items))...)\n        $(clean_body...)\n        $((define_constructor(struct_items[c.return_type_name], c)\n            for c in values(constructor_items))...)\n    end)))\nend","title":"@compose"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Templates","page":"Implementation","text":"Templates are defined by adding an argument list to a @compose module definition, made possible by the following catch in the @compose macro.\n\n#| id: catch-template-definition\nif !isempty(body) && @capture(body[1], {raw_parameters__})\n    parameters = raw_parameters .|> make_parameter\n    template = ModuleTemplate(mod_name, __module__, parameters, body[2:end])\n    return esc(Expr(:toplevel, :(const $mod_name = $template)))\nend\n\nThen the @mixin pass expands templates. A module with a unique name is generated in the parent space and imported into the mixin by the original template name.\n\n#| id: template-expansion\nstruct MixinTemplate <: AbstractMixin\n    instance_name::Symbol\n    template_name::Symbol\n    arguments::Vector{Any}\nend\n\nname(t::MixinTemplate) = t.instance_name\n\nfunction eval_to_expression(mod::Module, t::MixinTemplate)\n    template = Core.eval(mod, t.template_name)\n\n    arguments = t.arguments .|> eval_argument(mod)\n    bound_vars = bind(template.parameters, arguments)\n\n    module_expr = Expr(:toplevel, :(@compose module $(t.instance_name)\n        $(to_expression.(bound_vars)...)\n        $(template.body...)\n    end))\n    @eval(template.environment, $(module_expr))\n\n    return :(using ..$(nameof(template.environment)): $(t.instance_name) as $(t.template_name))\nend\n\nfunction parse_mixin_arg(expr)\n    if @capture(expr, name_{raw_arguments__})\n        instance_name = gensym(name)\n        return MixinTemplate(instance_name, name, raw_arguments)\n    elseif expr isa Symbol\n        return MixinModule(expr)\n    end\n    error(\"illegal mixin target: $(expr)\")\nend","title":"Templates"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Tests","page":"Implementation","text":"#| file: test/TemplateMixinSpec.jl\nmodule TMSpec\n    using ModuleMixins: @compose\n    @compose module A{T}\n        struct S\n            x::T\n        end\n    end\n\n    @compose module B\n        @mixin A{Int}\n    end\n\n    @compose module C\n        @mixin A{Float64}\n    end\n\n    export A, B, C\nend\n\n@testset \"ModuleMixins.TemplateMixins\" begin\n    using .TMSpec\n\n    @test fieldtype(B.S, 1) === Int\n    @test fieldtype(C.S, 1) === Float64\nend","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Constructors","page":"Implementation","text":"#| file: src/Constructors.jl\nmodule Constructors\n\nusing MacroTools: @capture\nusing .Iterators: repeated\n\nimport ..Passes: Pass, pass, no_match\nimport ..Structs: Struct\n\n<<constructor-pass>>\n\nend\n\nOnce we have several structs in place, we might want to generate one type from another. Suppose we have an Input struct and a State struct, and we want to automatically compose an initial_state function. We can do this if we have a function state_field(input::Input) returning the initial state for some field of State. We also may have the situation that we want to compute several fields in one go for efficiency.\n\n@constructor function initial_state(input::Input)::State[state_var1, state_var2]\n    return (\n        state_var1 = 42,\n        state_var2 = \"pangalactic gargleblaster\"\n    )\nend\n\n#| file: test/ConstructorsSpec.jl\nmodule ConstructorTest\n    using ModuleMixins\n\n    @compose module CtA\n        struct S\n            x\n        end\n\n        @constructor make_s()::S[x] = (x = 5,)\n    end\n\n    @compose module CtB\n        @mixin CtA\n\n        struct S\n            y\n            z\n        end\n\n        @constructor function make_s()::S[y, z]\n            (y = 7, z = 9)\n        end\n    end\nend\n\n@testset \"ModuleMixins.Constructors\" begin\n    using .ConstructorTest: CtA, CtB\n    using ModuleMixins\n\n    @test CtA.make_s() == CtA.S(5)\n    @test CtB.make_s() == CtB.S(5, 7, 9)\nend","title":"Constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#Implementation-3","page":"Implementation","text":"The following defines a macro that converts that syntax into usable information to compose a larger constructor.\n\n#| id: constructor-pass\nstruct Constructor\n    name::Symbol\n    arg_names::Vector{Symbol}\n    return_type_name::Symbol\n    parts::Vector{Pair{Vector{Symbol}, Expr}}\nend\n\nfunction Base.:+(a::Constructor, b::Constructor)\n    @assert a.name == b.name \"names should be the same $(a.name), $(b.name)\"\n    @assert a.arg_names == b.arg_names\n    @assert a.return_type_name == b.return_type_name\n    @assert isdisjoint(first.(a.parts), first(b.parts))\n\n    return Constructor(\n        a.name, a.arg_names, a.return_type_name,\n        vcat(a.parts, b.parts))\nend\n\nBase.fieldnames(c::Constructor) = vcat(first.(c.parts)...)\n\nnamed_tuple_keys(::Type{NamedTuple{names, types}}) where {names, types} = names\nnamed_tuple_keys(::Type{NamedTuple{names, <:types}}) where {names, types} = names\narg_name(arg::Symbol) = arg\narg_name(expr::Expr) = begin\n    @capture(expr, name_::atype_)\n    name\nend\n\nfunction parse_constructor(f)\n    @assert (\n        @capture(f, function name_(args__)::return_type_name_[fields__] body__ end) ||\n        @capture(f, name_(args__)::return_type_name_[fields__] = body__)\n    ) \"constructor expression doesn't match short or long form function:\\n $f\"\n    n_args = length(args)\n    arg_names = [arg_name(a) for a in args]\n    expr = :(function ($(arg_names...),) $(body...) end)\n    return Constructor(name, arg_names, return_type_name, [fields => expr])\nend\n\nWe can turn this into a Pass, so that the @constructor macro gets integrated into @compose.\n\n#| id: constructor-pass\nstruct CollectConstructorPass <: Pass\n    items::IdDict{Symbol, Constructor}\nend\n\nfunction pass(p::CollectConstructorPass, expr)\n    @capture(expr, @constructor constructor_expr_) || return no_match\n    data = parse_constructor(constructor_expr)\n\n    key = data.name\n    if key in keys(p.items)\n        p.items[key] += data\n    else\n        p.items[key] = data\n    end\n\n    return nothing\nend\n\nfunction define_constructor(s::Struct, c::Constructor)\n    @assert s.name == c.return_type_name\n    @assert issetequal(fieldnames(s), fieldnames(c)) \"constructor should construct all fields of struct, expected $(fieldnames(s)), got $(fieldnames(c))\"\n    return :(function $(c.name)($(c.arg_names...),)\n        $((:(($(first(p)...),) = ($(last(p)))($(c.arg_names...),))\n           for p in c.parts)...)\n        $(c.return_type_name)($(fieldnames(s)...),)\n    end)\nend","title":"Implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"50-implementation/#For-each","page":"Implementation","text":"The @for_each macro is meant for situations where you want to call a certain member function for each module that has it defined. Our use case: we have several components that need to write different bits of information to an output file. Each component defines a write(io, data) method. In our composed model, we can now call:\n\n@for_each(P->P.write(io, data), PARENTS)\n\n#| id: test-compose-toplevel\nmodule Common\n    export AbstractData\n    abstract type AbstractData end\nend\n\n@compose module WriterA\n    using ..Common\n\n    @kwdef struct Data <: AbstractData\n        a::Int\n    end\n\n    function write(io::IO, data::AbstractData)\n        println(io, data.a)\n    end\nend\n\n@compose module WriterB\n    using ..Common\n    @mixin WriterA\n\n    @kwdef struct Data <: AbstractData\n        b::Int\n    end\n\n    function write(io::IO, data::AbstractData)\n        println(io, data.b)\n    end\nend\n\n@compose module WriterC\nend\n\n@compose module WriterABC\n    using ModuleMixins\n    @mixin WriterB, WriterC\n\n    function write(io::IO, data::AbstractData)\n        @for_each(P->P.write(io, data), PARENTS)\n    end\nend\n\n#| id: test-compose\n@testset \"for-each\" begin\n    io = IOBuffer(write=true)\n    data = WriterABC.Data(a = 42, b = 23)\n    WriterABC.write(io, data)\n    @test String(take!(io)) == \"23\\n42\\n\"\nend\n\n#| id: for-each\n\"\"\"\n    substitute_top_level(var, val, mod, expr)\n\nTakes a syntax object `expr` and substitutes every occurence of\nmodule `var` for `val`, only if the resulting object is actually\npresent in module `mod`. The `mod` module should correspond with\na lookup of `val` in the caller's namespace.\n\"\"\"\nfunction substitute_top_level(var, val, mod, expr)\n    postwalk(function (x)\n        @capture(x, gen_.item_) || return x\n        if gen === var\n            if item in names(mod, all=true)\n                return Expr(:., val, QuoteNode(item))\n            else\n                return Returns(nothing)\n            end\n        end\n        return x\n    end, expr)\nend\n\n\"\"\"\n    @for_each(M -> M.method(), lst::Vector{Symbol})\n\nCalls `method()` for each module in `lst` that actually implements\nthat method. Here `lst` should be a vector of symbols that are all\nin the current module's namespace.\n\"\"\"\nmacro for_each(_fun, _lst)\n    @assert @capture(_fun, var_ -> expr_)\n\n    function replace_call_parent(p)\n        mod = Core.eval(__module__, p)\n        substitute_top_level(var, p, mod, expr)\n    end\n\n    lst = Core.eval(__module__, _lst)\n    esc(:(begin\n        $((replace_call_parent(p) for p in lst)...)\n    end))\nend\n\n","title":"For-each"},{"category":"section","location":"10-introduction/#Introduction","page":"Introduction","text":"The basis for ModuleMixins is a single macro: @compose. We use that macro to expand a system of interconnected struct definitions in a modular fashion. Each composed module may be refered to as a component. In the following example, we have one component A with a struct S and a second component B that expands on S.\n\nusing ModuleMixins\n\n@compose module A\n  struct S\n    a\n  end\nend\n\n@compose module B\n  @mixin A\n\n  struct S\n    b\n  end\nend\n\nfieldnames(B.S)\n(:a, :b)\n\nA struct within a composed module can be mutable and/or @kwdef, abstract base types are also forwarded. All using and const statements are forwarded to the derived module, so that field types still compile.\n\nusing ModuleMixins\n\n@compose module A\n  const V = Vector{Int}\n  struct S\n    a::V\n  end\nend\n\n@compose module B\n  @mixin A\nend\n\ntypeof(B.S([42]).a)\nVector{Int64}\u001b[90m (alias for \u001b[39m\u001b[90mArray{Int64, 1}\u001b[39m\u001b[90m)\u001b[39m","title":"Introduction"},{"category":"section","location":"10-introduction/#Diamond-pattern","page":"Introduction","text":"For some use cases you could get away with using more traditional composition techniques The following pattern of multiple inheritence should work:\n\nusing ModuleMixins: @compose\n\n@compose module A\n    struct S a::Int end\nend\n\n@compose module B\n    @mixin A\n    struct S b::Int end\nend\n\n@compose module C\n    @mixin A\n    struct S c::Int end\nend\n\n@compose module D\n    @mixin B, C\n    struct S d::Int end\nend\n\nfieldnames(D.S)\n(:a, :b, :c, :d)\n\nThe type D.S now has fields a, b, c and d.","title":"Diamond pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"10-introduction/#Motivation-from-OOP","page":"Introduction","text":"Julia is not an object oriented programming (OOP) language. In general, when one speaks of object orientation a mix of a few related concepts is meant:\n\nCompartimenting program state.\nMessage passing between entities.\nAbstraction over interfaces.\nInheritence or composition.\n\nWhere in other languages these concepts are mostly covered by classes, in Julia most of the patterns that are associated with OOP are implemented using multiple dispatch.\n\nThe single concept that is not covered by the Julia language or the standard library is inheritance or object composition. Suppose we have two types along with some methods (in the real world these would be much more extensive structs):\n\nstruct A\n    x::Int\nend\n\namsg(a::A) = \"Hello $(a.x)\"\n\nstruct B\n    y::Int\nend\n\nbmsg(b::B) = \"Goodbye $(b.y)\"\n\nNow, for some reason you want to compose those types so that amsg and bmsg can be called on our new type.\n\nstruct C\n    x::Int\n    y::Int\nend\n\namsg(c::C) = amsg(A(c.x))\nbmsg(c::C) = bmsg(B(c.y))\n\nThere are some downsides to this: we needed to copy data from C into the more primitive types A and B. We could get around this by removing the types from the original method implementations. Too strict static typing can be a bad thing in Julia!\n\nAn alternative approach would be to define C differently:\n\nstruct C\n    a::A\n    b::B\nend\n\nWe would need to abstract over member access using getter and setter methods. When objects grow a bit larger, this type of compositon comes with a lot of boilerplate code. In Julia this is synonymous to: we need macros.\n\nThere we have it: if we want any form of composition or inheritance in our types, we need macros to support us.","title":"Motivation from OOP"},{"category":"section","location":"10-introduction/#Constructors","page":"Introduction","text":"When you can use inheritance to compose larger objects, it would also be nice if we can construct the larger object without additional code. Suppose we have some Input and a State struct. It is good to put common definitions in a Common module.\n\nusing ModuleMixins\n\nmodule Common\n    using Unitful\n    export @u_str, Seconds, Kilograms, Meters, Radians, RadiansPerSecond\n\n    const Seconds = typeof(1.0u\"s\")\n    const Kilograms = typeof(1.0u\"kg\")\n    const Meters = typeof(1.0u\"m\")\n    const Radians = typeof(1.0u\"rad\")\n    const RadiansPerSecond = typeof(1.0u\"rad/s\")\nend\nMain.Common\n\nOur very first component stores the Input inside the State for later reference:\n\n@compose module ModelBase\n    @kwdef struct Input\n    end\n\n    struct State{I}\n        input::I\n    end\n\n    @constructor initial_state(input)::State[input] = (input = input,)\nend\nMain.ModelBase\n\nNotice that we must give the return type of the constructor. All later additions to the constructor must have the same parameter names.\n\nOur first real component introduces time:\n\n@compose module Time\n    @mixin ModelBase\n    using ..Common\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        delta_t::Seconds = 0.1u\"s\"\n        steps::Int = 1000\n    end\n\n    struct State\n        step::Int\n    end\n\n    @constructor initial_state(input)::State[step] = (step = 0,)\n\n    time(state) = state.step * state.input.delta_t\nend\nMain.Time\n\nWe may model a pendulum:\n\n@compose module Pendulum\n    using ..Common\n    @mixin Time\n\n    @kwdef struct Input\n        length::Meters = 1.0u\"m\"\n        mass::Kilograms = 1.0u\"kg\"\n        initial_angle::Radians = 30.0u\"deg\"\n    end\n\n    struct State\n        angle::Radians\n        angular_velocity::RadiansPerSecond\n    end\n\n    @constructor function initial_state(input)::State[angle, angular_velocity]\n        (angle = input.initial_angle,\n         angular_velocity = 0.0u\"rad/s\")\n    end\nend\nMain.Pendulum\n\nWe can now create an initial state using the default values for input.\n\nPendulum.initial_state(Pendulum.Input())\nMain.Pendulum.State{Main.Pendulum.Input}(Main.Pendulum.Input(0.1 s, 1000, 1.0 m, 1.0 kg, 0.5235987755982988 rad), 0, 0.5235987755982988 rad, 0.0 rad s^-1)\n\n","title":"Constructors"}]
}
